{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/408983-fanmade-dark-era-libert\u00e9-\u00e9galit\u00e9-vitae","id":408983,"title":"Fanmade Dark Era: Libert\u00e9, \u00c9galit\u00e9, Vitae","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-02-17T08:20:01-08:00","id":408983,"title":"Fanmade Dark Era: Libert\u00e9, \u00c9galit\u00e9, Vitae","content":"I've been inspired the be work on Dark Eras to produce my own, and I'll be starting a game set in the period next week.<br /> <br /> You can find the PDF attached, but here is the full text for those who prefer to read it in browser:<br /> <b>Liberti\u00e9 \u2022\u00c9galiti\u00e9 \u2022Vitae</b><br /><br /> A city on the edge of chaos, Paris is a capital of squalor and suffering as the 18th Century draws to a close. While for hundreds of years the regular procession of plagues kept the peasant population in check there has been no great sickness in generations, and the numbers of the underclass swells in the city. Now Paris is home to almost 650,000 people, and only a bare ten thousand are of the first and second estates, the clergy and the nobility. The rest call the third their home and make up the peasantry. As the population has swollen the balance of production and consumption has become ever more unstable and dangerous, the poor roads and tracks making it impossible to import grain to the city at anything other than extortionate prices, and so the populace relies totally on the agriculture of the city surrounds themselves.<br /> Only the regulation of bread prices keeps the city from starving and even then, when a bad harvest occurs, the peasantry riots nearly tearing the city apart, lynching bakers, stealing bread and burning down buildings that have stood for half a millennium. The clergy and nobility pay no taxes and take all that they want, and are impeachable in the courts of the land. The young king and queen, Louis XVI and Marie Antoinette, luxuriate in the palace of Versailles far from the heavy masses, totally isolated from the pain and suffering of the lower classes. King Louis, though most incompetent, refuses to appoint important ministerial figures claiming his right to rule the country directly and believing that it is his duty to do so.<br /> His father, and his father's father, wasted the riches of France on a series of petty wars and disputes, the lands and monies of France slowly eaten away. The rich have gotten richer, and the poor poorer as they are squeezed more and more to pay for the excesses of the first and second estates. The people call for a three house parliament to represent the common man, but such pleas are ignored. France teeters on bankruptcy, a matter which is not helped by Marie Antoinette's lavish spending, and the vast sums Louis sends to aid the American Wars of Independence.<br /> It is June 1787, and the hungry gap is beginning, when the harvests are yet to be brought in but the grain stores are running low. Bread is scarce and the city boils and fumes in the heat and the poverty, a powder keg that somehow never explodes.<br /> As above, so below.<br /> The three estates of the dead struggle against each other just as the kine do. The nobility of the night and the followers of Longinus claim the first and second estates, with the rest relegated to the third. Those kindred who are not embraced into noble lines, or who cannot worm their way into the church, are left to rot, forced to pay the blood taxes of the nobles and given nothing in return. A bare handful of bloodlines hold all the power and the rest scramble to just survive.<br /> As the kine population bloats as do the leeches that feed from them, hundreds of the dead hanging like parasites from the city. With so many deaths through crime, starvation and a thousand other evils, the peasantry of the city are a never-ending buffet for the kindred whose predations are always overlooked. Yet fury and resentment ferments amongst the downtrodden masses of the dead, just as they do amongst the living. The kindred might have blood aplenty but they lack safety in a city which burns on a yearly basis, and which is ripped apart by riots almost monthly. They look at the sturdy chambers and manors of their betters and then plot amongst themselves.<br /> Just as the pamphleteers of the mortal world fuel the ever-encroaching revolution, so do the radicals of the dead urge their fellows to action. The name Emmanuel Baptiste Carth is upon the lips of the many, and soon they will be bound together into a great movement whose actions can only result in blood and fire. <b>Theme and Mood</b><br /><br /> The theme is <i>Divided Loyalties</i>. Pulled in different directions by loyalty to one\u2019s Clan, Covenant and place the social hierarchy of Paris, all kindred must face up to the fact that no choice is simple, and no choice does not betray someone.<br /> The mood is <i>Pushing Back against Tyranny.</i> The city\u2019s mortals are wracked by constant riots and uprisings, and though the dead must hide their fury more carefully it is there nonetheless. Every collection of corv\u00e9e, every judgement of the Prince, and every excess of the nobility makes that anger deeper, harsher and burn fiercer. <b>What Has Come Before</b><br /><br /> Paris has stood for more than two thousand years in one form or another and the kindred have been here through every one of those years. Their history is complex and closely intertwined with the lives of the mortals they rely on. <b>Raised from Nothing: Roman Lutetia (52BC \u2013 486AD)</b><br /><br /> There has been a settlement on the banks of the Seine for longer than mankind remembers, and one it was the home of the Parisii who ruled the lands they settled before the birth of Christ. The true history of the city begins, though, when the Romans came and made it their own, naming it Lutetia. Vercingetorix, the most famous of the Gallic chiefs, was defeated and Titus Labienus claimed the city for his own. They rebuilt it, the streets aligning to a regular grid in the style of Roman settlements further south, and it was soon an important place of the occupying forces. Where the living go, of course, the dead are soon to follow.<br /> The Legio Mortuum of the Camarilla were the first kindred to arrive according to the legends of the dead, an extension of the will of Senex who ruled the whole world of the dead. The Legion found the native vampires, the rough barbarian tribesmen, to be amenable to their rule, and it was not long before they made their way back along the trade routes to the city where all roads lead. For Lutetia it was a time of prosperity both for kindred and kine as the military outpost became a hub for trade and then a place in its own right. The amphitheatre on the outskirts served mortal entertainments during the day, but hosted kindred gatherings at night. It seemed the power of the Camarilla could not wane, and neither could its living child Rome.<br /> But all things change. Whisper of troubles back in the eternal city began to reach the ears of Roman kindred in Gaul, and soon a steady stream left the security of the small settlement and headed back to Rome to gather allies and shore up old debts. The living were the same and the power of Rome was fading, soon to fall away completely as the native peoples asserted themselves once more. By the late 4<sup>th</sup> century Rome was no longer the power it was and the city was renamed Paris, taking its name from the people who once lived there. The Julii were dead and the kindred of Gaul once more ruled themselves. <b>Glory of Charlemagne: The Early Christian Period (AD 509-885)</b><br /><br /> Time passed and the remains of the old empire began to reform and rebuild and Paris once again rose to prominence when Clovis the Frank, the first kind of the Merovingian dynasty, made the city his capital. The native Gangrel were no longer alone in the city by now, joined the Ventrue who had travelled from the east, and the worm Nosferatu who had clambered out of their dens in the soil and stood blinking in the light of the moon with their fellows. The city had a feeling of growth, that great things awaited it, and the kindred were no different. The first Prince of Paris rose to power and ruled with the support not only of the burgeoning Invictus, but also the Lancea et Sanctum whose faith had spread north in recent decades.<br /> The kings of the Franks ruled well for the most part, but Paris remained small and constrained to the islands that were the best form of fortification against invades available. Slowly work began upon building a greater place to live, but they came to little until Childeric III was deposed by the Pope and Pepin the Short was made king, the first of the Carolingians whose greatest scion was yet to arise.<br /> Under Charlemagne the lands were united, his armies conquering great swathes of territory and though Paris remained important it waned in influence as the emperor moved his capital to Aachen. The kindred looked upon his works and were jealous, and worked even harder to dominate their fellows, their influence spreading out from their traditional holdings across all of northern Europe. They were joined for the first time by the Daeva who hailed from the south and east, and the first noble lines of Paris were founded, tradition dictating that each new Prince would be drawn from one of these noble families.<br /> The death of Charlemagne led to a time of fading fortunes as the great empire that had been claimed collapsed. The influence and links the kindred had made to much effort to establish began to falter and within decades the influence of the Invictus was once more localised and isolated. The kindred of Paris withdrew, consumed by petty squabbles and infighting, and for kine and kindred alike the dark ages closed in. <b>The Third Race of Kings: The Rule of the House of Capet (AD 987 \u2013 1328)</b><br /><br /> After many years of confusion and conflict a new King of the Franks rose to power, and the House of Capet became the dominant noble force in France. This \u201cthird race of kings\u201d returned power to Paris and the city swelled with people and wealth, soon becoming the most prosperous city in the nation. The kindred\u2019s numbers grew as well and soon a full and formal court was established, and the modern noble lines emerged: the Rome, the Fortier, the Chastain and the Lapointe. By the end of the 12<sup>th</sup> century Paris has become a city of wonders and lights.<br /> Notre Dame Cathedral was built and the University of Paris established as the power of the city grew, and it became a centre of philosophical and theological learning. Commerce and finance blossomed and money flowed into the city with great speed and the league of river traders was formed to control the transit of goods. The Louvre was built and the golden age of Paris began.<br /> The kindred flourished too and by the beginning of the 14<sup>th</sup> century nearly a hundred vampires made their home in the city, feeding off the swelling ranks of mortals and their influence in kine affairs growing year on year. France became not only a great nation of thinkers and merchants but one of soldiers and sailors, and the kindred spread out with the armies of France taking their model of stable society with them. Many courts of Europe came to follow the model of Paris, with a handful of noble lines maintaining control over a population, aided by the church of Longinus. <b>Plague and War: The Period of Uncertainty (AD 1348 \u2013 1594)</b><br /><br /> But not all was smooth and easy for the city, and when the Hundred Years was erupted the city was captured by the English and Burgundians. With religious tensions increasingly troublesome France was wracked with strife, and even when the city was reclaimed almost a century later it was still torn apart by sectarian violence. The kindred could not escape the intrusion of Protestantism either, and as it became every more dominant amongst the kine the church of Longinus was forced to respond. Religious strife weakened the rule of the nobles but they managed to cling to power, trying to ride a wave of discontent that fractured alliances of all kinds.<br /> France was literally torn apart by the conflict but Paris remained a stronghold of Catholicism, and the St Bartholomew\u2019s Day massacre where thousands of Protestants were killed was a sign of the devotion not only of Catholics but also of the traditionalist wing of the Lancea et Sanctum, their internal conflict spilling out in the lives of mortals as so often was the case. Slowly the city began to rebuild and the kindred reunify and by the time Henri IV converted to Catholicism and ended the wars of religions, the dead had already resolved their own issues. The traditionalists and the Catholics had won, and Paris was whole once more. <b>Palaces and Parlours: The Years of Excess (AD 1671 \u2013 1774)</b><br /><br /> With France healing the kings were determined to show that the nation was still proud and unbroken, and worked hard to build up their capital as a symbol of their strength. The city was remodelled into something more beautiful than ever before and statues, academies and theatres all sprung up in great numbers. The dead could not avoid such a change and embraced the aesthetic spirit of the city, becoming great patrons of the arts and sciences and investing their own influence to make the city ever greater. L\u2019Academie du Serpent was established at this time and became known throughout the courts of Europe as a centre for kindred learning.<br /> With the great projects of the age draining the coffers of France the nation began to find itself relying more and more upon their status as traders and soldiers, and soon their colonial efforts bore fruit. Their holdings overseas, however, were a drain on the finances more than a benefit, with the nobility taking the profits of such ventures for themselves. Paris itself became a place of imbalance and division as the population grew at a phenomenal rate and the city expanded with great speed, but the working classes became poorer and poorer. Despite being the financial capital of Europe the city housed huge numbers of suffering peasants, their poverty crushing them and never letting them better themselves. <b>Louis and Marie: Recent Years and the Rule of the King (AD 1774 \u2013 1787)</b><br /><br /> The ascension of Louis XVI to the throne was a turn for the worse again. Crowned at the age of 19 and seen as young, inexperienced and timid by both the nobility and the populace in general, his was a rule that encountered difficulty from the very beginning. Even before he became king he had been unpopular, in part due to his difficult marriage to Marie Antoinette, who was seen as a puppet of Austrian influence, and their inability to consummate their marriage or produce children.<br /> Despite this things seemed a little brighter when he did become king as he began to push for reform and move towards enlightenment ideals, something that resonated well with both the impoverished lower classes and the ambitious middle classes. However, his proposed reforms caused fury amongst the nobility and he was forced to drop them, something seen as a betrayal by the people and as a sign of his weakness as a ruler by his political opponents.<br /> Determined to make a name for himself, Louis sunk much of the nation\u2019s remaining finances into supporting the North American colonists in their attempts to win independence from Great Britain, which made the country popular in the new world but prompted a disastrous financial collapse. This combined with his indecisiveness and inability to push reform led to the people of France increasingly seeming him as a symbol of the tyranny of the Ancien R\u00e9gime, the social structure of the Estates that led to the poor having few to no rights at all.<br /> Despite the upheaval of in the mortal world, the kindred of Paris were little affected by these excesses and the unpopularity of the royalty. With the population of Paris growing ever larger the kindred found themselves in possession of a seemingly limitless supply of blood, and the increasing chaos of the city and discontent of the poor served as a perfect aide to the masquerade. However, this increase in resources has led to a much larger kindred population, and in response a much more unequal society amongst the dead. The younger kindred, almost all of whom are relegated to the Third Estate, railed against their lack of power and the increasingly harsh corv\u00e9e demanded by their regents. Motivated by mortal ideas of political change and enlightenment concepts of equality and justice, they began to form loose alliances of kindred radicals looking to change their own government just as the mortals long for change themselves. The brutality and sometimes inept leadership of Le Requin did not help matters during this time. <b>What is to Come</b><br /><br /> With the storming of the Bastille only two years away, France hangs on the edge of a precipice. Though Louis XIV is hugely unpopular and the country is in turmoil, enough power remains entrenched with the nobility for them to hang on for some time yet. The populace retains some hope yet that things will improve, however, as comptroller Charles Alexandre de Calonne is in the process of proposing tax reforms that would ease the burden on the poor and introduce some equality to the system. It won\u2019t be long, however, before the nobility and the church begin to fight back, and action which will eventually result in the summoning of the Assembly of Notables. When they refuse to act to resolve the crisis the King will call the Estates-General, a sign that the monarchy was losing control.<br /> The meeting of the Estates-General in 1789 would fall into chaos quickly \u2013 using procedural tricks to tie up the much larger Third Estate the First and Second Estates took control of the meeting. In response the Third Estate will declare themselves the National Assembly and began to attempt to conduct the nation\u2019s affairs with or without the clergy and the nobility. The King would attempt to shut down the assembly but this only strengthened their resolve, and soon riots would break out across France. In Paris the mobs stormed the Bastille, a symbol of oppression, and the revolution would truly begin.<br /> With unrest only growing and peasant uprisings common, change would come quickly \u2013 the national assembly will reject feudalism, reduce the power of the church and nobility and attempt to force the monarchy to become constitutional. Fleeing Paris and Versailles only highlights in the eyes of the people the guilt of the King, and he is arrested. Though for months the assembly argues back and forth about what to do with him, they eventually disbanded the monarchy and established a republic. A year later, in 1793, he will be executed for conspiracy against the public safety. War breaks out between France and England, and counter-revolutions are attempted. The reign of terror will grip the country under the guidance of Robespierre, before he is arrested and executed by his opponents.<br /> The country will stutter and struggle for ten years of revolution as decrees are passed and the fortunes of the nation rise and fall. In 1799 Napoleon Bonaparte will stage a coup and install the consulate, before becoming dictator and then emperor. And France will never be the same again.<br /> For the kindred the years are as bleak. With chaos and terror gripping Paris their unending food supply turns into a terrible threat, and fires and battles destroy havens and risk the masquerade. The kindred struggle between themselves, but if the radicals manage to overcome those who dominate them as the mortals do\u2026 that is lost in the mists. <b>Paris Tonight</b><br /><br /> Paris is a large and complex city with many unique or unusual institutions. Though the kindred share many of their political and social structures with other domains, a number are either unique to Paris or have special significance in the City of Lights. <b>Noble and Common Lines</b><br /><br /> Though clan is still of importance to many in the city the real division is by <i>bloodline</i> \u2013 the direct lineage of a kindred and who their sire and grandsire were. Those who are unaware of their lineage are considered to not just be lesser than other kindred, but verging on monstrous, as a sense of one\u2019s place in society is imparted by the vitae that flows in their veins. These families of the dead can be divided into noble and common lines depending on their historical status in the city, but exactly why any particular line is considered noble and another base has been forgotten for centuries.<br /> The noble lines have distinct rights that the commoners do not, and it is an important facet of the city\u2019s politics. Only those of the noble bloodlines can control territory in the city and have tenants, meaning that they are the sole beneficiaries of corv\u00e9e and have the rights of the landlord of their tenants. Nobles are perfectly within their rights to use disciplines on common kindred, but not each other.<br /> Commoners find their rights much more restrictive. Not only must they have a landlord and thus pay rent, and are banned from owning territory outright, they may not move between territories or relocate their havens without permission. Travelling to visit others in the city is frowned upon though many still do so via the necropolis. Commoners must petition their landlord for the right to embrace, and in theory to create ghouls (though this is often overlooked in practice). For a commoner to use a discipline on a noble kindred is a breach of the law of the city and punished harshly if discovered. It is also illegal for a commoner to blood bond a noble kindred, and if this is discovered the commoner is usually placed in torpor or executed for the slight.<br /> Put together these restrictions mean that the noble lines have almost total power over the commoners in their territories, and this causes considerable strain. The driving force between much of the tension of the city is caused by the discrepancy in rights between noble and common kindred. <b>Districts and Barons</b><br /><br /> The city of Paris is divided up into a number of districts, each of which is controlled by one of the noble bloodlines and is made up of a number of territories ruled directly by a member of that bloodline. By convention only members of the Second Estate own territory, but there is at least one exception to this (as the Bishop Chastain has his own territory and has tenants living upon it). Each district is ruled by a regent (a senior member of a noble line) but they can in turn parcel out smaller territories to their underlings to rule as their own (and often owe them corv\u00e9e in turn).<br /> Those kindred who own territory are known as Barons or Propri\u00e9taire (\u201cLandlords\u201d) and have certain rights of their tenants. The most important is, of course, the collection of corv\u00e9e, but they also have the right to request their aid in times of crisis or danger and have them report any possible masquerade breaches within their territory. In return they are supposed to assist their tenants with any threats to the city, and protect them from interlopers and poachers (how well they actually do this is up for debate).<br /> <b>\u00cele de la Cit\u00e9</b> is one of the two islands at the heart of the city, and is the very oldest part of the city. It is ruled by the Chastain, and the regent is Bishop Chastain. The Carmichael number amongst their tenants and the relationship between the two is tense. The territory is home to <i>La Sainte-Chapelle </i>(a gothic chapel which houses part of the crown of thorns), La Conciergerie (a royal palace and prison), and Notre-Dame de Paris (an ancient cathedral of some note).<br /> <b>\u00cele Saint-Louis</b> is the other island in the Seine, and is primarily residential, and the richest area of the city by some way. It is territory claimed by the Rome, and the regent here is Amaury Rome, the eldest kindred of the dynasty. It is home to the Sarkozi brood, who are the most loyal servants of the Rome. It has no major landmarks, but instead is home to many elegant manors and houses.<br /> <b>La Rive Droite </b>is a large territory covering much of the city to the north of the Seine, and contains much of the more substantial residential districts and a large number of the historic landmarks of the city, and much of the royal presence in the city. Its regent is Georges Fortier and the tenants include the other wing of the Carmichael, and there are strong and amicable bonds between the two families - it is one of the few territories where the tenants can count on the support of their lords. It includes the <i>Royal Library of Paris</i>, the <i>Louvre Palace</i>, the <i>H\u00f4tel de Bourgogne</i>, and <i>Temple</i> (a fortress once run by the Knights Templar).<br /> <b>La Rive Gauche</b> is the largest territory within the city itself, and covers almost the entire south side of the river. It is by far the poorest area of the city and is rife with crime that makes it very difficult. It is also the locations of the majority of the slums of the city, and it houses a huge kine population. As such, though it is hardly elegant, it is prime territory, and the prize holding of the Rome family - its regent is Comtesse Giselle Rome, and its tenants include the Moreau brood and the fallen Lapointe.<br /> <b>Quartier Latin</b> is a small but select district which surrounds the University of Paris, and is named for the fact that Latin is very commonly heard throughout the territory. Its major landmark is the University itself, which dominates the territory. The Quartier Latin is a strange territory for it falls between the control of two different regents and is ruled instead by the Courtmanche (who are not noble). This strange land of outlawry is allowed to continue both because of the difficulty of enforcing the law on the Comte Courtmanche, but also because of the usefulness of L\u2019Academie du Serpent.<br /> <b>Champs-\u00c9lys\u00e9es</b> is the newest area of the city, built around the comparatively recently laid down road that bears its name. It is a lively and bohemian district, known for its theatres and cafes, and for its rowdy atmosphere. The regent of the district is Ethan Fortier who is relatively convivial with his rule, and his tenants include the Roux brood, which makes for an exciting if unpredictable mix.<br /> <b>Les Marais</b> is a small residential district located to the north of the river, and on the edge of the city. It is traditionally the residence of the aristocracy and contains a plethora of churches and mansions. Its regent was once Christophe Lapointe but since his execution has fallen into the hands of Anna Chastain. The Favager are among the tenants of the territory, though exactly how many dwell there is uncertain, and Anna has found trying to extract service from them infuriatingly difficult.<br /> <b>Versailles</b> is the largest territory in the whole domain, for it is not truly part of the city - instead it covers all of the countryside around the city, the small towns and villages therein, and of course Versailles itself. It is ruled directly by the Prince himself, and no common kindred are allowed within, the Prince taking corv\u00e9e instead from the nobility. The court itself is the most prominent landmark in the territory, and all that really matters to its lord. <b>Corv\u00e9e and Laisse</b><br /><br /> The Second and First Estates maintain their rule through the collection of corv\u00e9e, or <i>service</i>. Corv\u00e9e is collected on an annual basis (usually at a fixed time in the year, but technical a baron can request it at any time if it has not yet been collected that year) by barons from those of the Third Estate who live in their territory, a sort of \u201crent\u201d which all of the peasantry are obliged to pay to their lords. This tribute can be collected in a variety of different ways, with the baron who is collecting it dictating the form it takes. According to the law of the city, corv\u00e9e must be collected as one of the following:<br /> <b>Travail pour le sang</b> (\u201cLabour for the Blood\u201d), which is any service which calls upon the vassal to collect vessels for the lord to feed from, or which requires them to clean up after a lord\u2019s feeding. This is rarely used by the nobility for the purpose of sustenance, but rather as a way to get access to important or influential mortals in order to blood bond or dominate them, and also as a way to have masquerade breaches or threats dealt with. As it is the fault of their vassal if a masquerade breach occurs due to them not \u201ccleaning up\u201d well enough after their excesses, this is often used to shift blame away from a sloppy aristocrat and onto the commoners in his territory.<br /> <b>Travail pour la puissance</b> (\u201cLabour for the Power\u201d), which his any service which calls upon the vassal to behave according to his low position. This can include acting as things like a messenger, porter, or a valet, or being loaned to another lord in any capacity, and is in effect indentured labour. Some nobles are lenient with the use of this form of corv\u00e9e, or deign not to use it at all, but others keep their tenants in almost year-round servitude by requiring their labour in perpetuity.<br /> <b>Corv\u00e9e sanglant</b> (\u201cBlood Service\u201d), which is any service or act which is humiliating, gross, or foul, whether or not it involves blood. This is such a broad category of activity that it can be twisted by a suitably motivated noble to mean almost anything they wish. However, common activities include violence (including murder of mortals), information gathering in unpleasant places or even just cleaning up after mortals or animals. It is used most often as a punishment with the intention of cowing the commoner into behaving better in future.<br /> <b>Loyer</b> (\u201cRent\u201d) is the final form in which corv\u00e9e can be collected, and involves a regular payment of some kind made to the vassal\u2019s lord. It usually takes the form of goods or money (and this can be an excellent revenue stream for the nobility if their vassals are wealthy), but can also be less physical things, such as information, knowledge or tuition.<br /> This system of taxation is the most common form of exchange within the city, but it does not handle exchanges between kindred of an equal social standing, or those who wish to take additional services from their vassals beyond their due corv\u00e9e. In such circumstances, the standard transaction is in <i>Laisse </i>(or \u201cLeash\u201d, as one is leashed to the debt). Laisse are official favours owed between kindred which must be recorded in the presence of a court official, who records it in the city\u2019s books, or Comptes. Laisse which are not registered are not law abiding, but most members of the Third Estate choose not to record them anyway and are generally very hesitant about not paying their debts. Laisse can vary greatly in value and can be kept for any length of time before being called in. Refusing to pay an officially recorded Laisse is a crime against the city, though it should be noted that this law is applied considerably more harshly to members of the Third Estate than the First or Second. There are four types of Laisse: Banal, Mineur, Majeur, and Fatal.<br /> <b>Laisse Banal </b>(or \u201cTrivial) are the meanest sort of favour, and take only a small amount of invested time and effort, such as a loan of equipment, or an introduction to another.<br /> <b>Laisse Mineur </b>(or \u201cMinor\u201d) are actually of considerable note, and represent a whole night's effort and work to fulfil, or lesser effort over a full month. Examples might include supporting another's political goals, a lesson in a common power of the blood, or protecting someone's possessions for a period. One Laisse Mineur is worth three Laisse Banal.<br /> <b>Laisse Majeur</b> (or \u201cMajor\u201d) are considerable debts which a massive effort and potentially considerable danger to pay off. It can call on a kindred's service for a full year, and might include adopting a kindred into your bloodline, giving up a valuable retainer, or accepting a partial blood bond to another. A Laisse Majeur is worth three Laisse Mineur.<br /> <b>Laisse Fatal </b>(or \u201cLife\u201d) are rarely exchanged, and cannot be imposed on another - it can only be taken on voluntarily. It is the supreme show of devotion and debt, and can be called upon to do almost anything, including sacrificing oneself. You cannot trade lesser laisse for a Laisse Fatal, or a Laisse Fatal for other types.<br /> Laisse can be exchanged and traded freely, with a set exchange rate. All such transactions must again be recorded with the court to be legally binding, but trades of Laisse Banal are very common without any records being taken. Some bloodlines make secondary records of Laisse to ensure they are kept. <b>The Clans</b><br /><br /> Blood and lineage is of great importance to the kindred of Paris and as such the clans make great efforts to work in a unified fashion. Despite this it is truly the bloodlines and lineages themselves that divide kindred more than actual clan, and as such few embrace the clan as a political entity. No clan has agreed upon a priscus for almost four hundred years and that seems unlikely to change in the near future. Instead the patriarchs and matriarchs of a clan come together to discuss matters when it is necessary. <b>The Ventrue</b><br /><br /> Dominated by the two noble dynasties of the city, the Ventrue are a powerful clan but a divided one. The Rome and the Fortier rule supreme wielding a disproportionate amount of power considering their small numbers, and some Ventrue take pride in this victory. Others are jealous and grasping, and some few in their desperation have even turned to the radicals \u2013 after all, winning is all that matters, not how you go about achieving your victory.<br /> The <b>Rome</b> are the most powerful dynasty in Paris, if not all of France, as they count amongst their number Le Requin himself, Henri Rome. What is more, the prince is not even the patriarch of their line, and Amaury Rome remains a potent if mysterious figure in city politics, shepherding his childe through the travails of political office. The line is very small, barely a dozen members in total, but a full third of them are elders with at least three centuries experience, and the dynasty actually manages to cooperate with remarkable efficiency. As a combination this is devastating, and it is perhaps no surprise that the Rome have a stranglehold on the city. Though they have extensive territories in the city they also favour the town of Versailles for its royal connections. Those not of noble blood are banned from entering the town, and of course that is where Henri holds his courts. The Rome family have considerable influence in the kine courts at Versailles and across the mortal nobility more generally giving them tremendous influence. Some even whisper that they have ties to the King and Queen themselves, though such influence would surely be skirting the limits of the masquerade.<br /> The other noble line in the city are the <b>Fortier</b> who can trace their lineage back six full generations, making them one of the oldest dynasties in the city. Their founder claims that he was embraced by a kindred of Rome, which would make them direct blood relatives of the Dead Julii \u2013 the truth of the matter is up for debate, but few dare to reject the notion in hearing of the Fortier themselves. Numbering a handful more than the Rome, the Fortier are a larger but less unified dynasty which if anything weakens their power \u2013 too many hands on the tiller make for lengthy journeys. It would be imprudent indeed, however, to dismiss them as simply second best to the Rome, for they have one of the largest and most comprehensive spy networks in the city, and tendrils of control flowing throughout the civil government of Paris itself. In particular they are known to have surprising influence in the kine courts which they ruthlessly exploit to their advantage. They are also known for their practice of \u201cpatronage voyages\u201d where small groups of younger kindred from the line are sent to domains across France in order to propagate Fortier influence and win favours and friends outside the city.<br /> Though not noble, the <b>Lestrange</b> are still an important and influential line of the Ventrue in Paris who hail originally from the eastern Europe. They were once the Ignatov but changed their name more than two centuries ago to ease their integration into the domain, and to put the fears of foreign invasion to rest. Despite this they retain much of the same Bulgarian pomp as their forefathers claimed, and for this reason they have naturally been attracted to influence within the armies of France. A respectable number of the line manage to gain admittance to the First Estate as members of the low clergy, but most can be found in the Third maintaining their bonds with soldiers and suppliers as best they can. <b>The Daeva</b><br /><br /> The Daeva were once the equals of the Venture in power, but their influence has waned over the last few decades and now their clan is in turmoil due to the fall of the Lapointe. They count a single noble line amongst their number and one former noble line now relegated to the peasantry. Hanging on to their position in the city through their influence in the First Estate, the clan is seen either as a hotbed of traitors or potential strong allies depending on who you ask.<br /> The <b>Chastain </b>are the last remaining noble line of Daeva in Paris, and they are determined to keep control. Though there are a number of members amongst the Second Estate (and their patriarch Jean Baptiste Chastain in encouraging more of the younger additions to stay within the nobility) the majority can be found in the First Estate and dominate the clergy of the Temple and Spear in the city. Though Bishop Chastain is a formidable figure he often struggles to coordination his kin as they constantly squabble amongst themselves and all are possessed of a strong will, a trait he looks for in potential embraces despite the difficulty it causes as he believes it will make the line stronger. The bloodline dominates the Catholic church in the city and has a huge amount of influence of the mortal population because of it.<br /> Once a noble line now relegated to the ranks of the peasantry, the <b>Lapointe</b> were always an awkward fit in the city. Kept aloft for decades by the cunning and power of their former patriarch Christopher Didier Lapointe, they were brought low when evidence of his intentions to betray Le Requin and support a radical reformation of the court, abolishing the division between noble and commoner forever. Though he was left for the sun his small brood were allowed to live and now make their residence in La Rive Gauche.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Lapointe Bloodline</u></b><br /> The Lapointe dynasty spreads its influence in less direct ways than many others, but its chains once forged are like iron. They are specialists in using the blood bond to control others, making them into soldiers for their armies. However, they are cunning, for the blood bonds they create do not bind their thralls to them directly, but rather to their cause.<br /> <b>Weakness:</b> The Lapointe's unique blood means they are more susceptible to the blood bonds of others. You cannot resist a blood bond, even by spending Willpower.<br /> <b>Gift:</b> The Lapointe's blood is particularly invigorating and compelling, and has a strange and potent effect on those who consume it.<ul><li>When an individual would normally suffer a blood bond to a member of the Lapointe, they instead suffer it to a particular goal or ideal determined by the Lapointe. The Lapointe must specify this ideal at the time the blood bond is first created, though if a bond fades and is remade, they can choose differently the next time. It could be something as general as &quot;Justice&quot; or as specific as &quot;the slaying of Bishop Marcus&quot;. These bonds, other than the changes noted below, operate exactly as normal bonds do.</li> <li>The First Stage blood bond now represents excitement and enthusiasm for the chosen ideal. It doesn't matter what they normally want, suddenly their focus shifts. When <i>anyone</i> takes a Social maneuver against the thrall where the end goal is to further that ideal, cause, or concept, their impression is considered one step higher. In addition, they gain a +1 die bonus on all Social actions and Discipline rolls against them, assuming against the end goal is in service of the ideal. Causing direct or indirect harm to the ideal or cause constitutes a breaking point at Humanity 5/Integrity-2</li> <li>The Second Stage blood bond now represents obsession and devotion to the chosen idea. Not only is it exciting, its fundamentally a good idea - it becomes and important facet of their belief in the world around them. The impression of social maneuvers (as above) is now considered <i>two</i> steps higher, and the bonus increases to +2. Causing harm to the ideal or cause is now a breaking point at Humanity 3 or Integrity on a -3.</li> <li>The Third Stage blood bond is simple and absolute zealotry, a totally consuming and obsessive need to further the cause. The impression on social maneuvers is always considered perfect, and the bonus rises to +3. Causing harm is now at Humanity 1/Integrity -5. Finally, all Discipline effects applied to the target that further the cause gain additional duration - scene becomes night, night becomes week, week becomes month, month becomes year. At the third stage it shatters all other bonds (even normal ones) and prevents further bonds from forming.</li> </ul><b>The Gangrel</b><br /><br /> The Gangrel are the peasantry of the surface, as it were, for their make their havens primarily above ground within the city. Favoured tenants of the Ventrue, they are rowdy and mostly ill-educated, but make up for it in sheer tenacity and numbers. They have a number of prominent lines, but none of them are noble.<br /> The <b>Sarkozi </b>are the most loyal of the Gangrel to their traditional masters, and the best equivalent of an army the city has. As such, they are rewarded heavily and indoctrinated upon embrace (or before), and have a terrifying skill in combat. Bruisers and thugs, they are usually used in the city when the high clans want to put down trouble amongst the peasantry. Their patriarch is Maurice Sarkozi, a Gangrel Elder of fearsome physical might, but who is not too bright.<br /> The <b>Roux </b>who are well-known for their influence amongst the mortal brothels and prostitutes, and who are also known for their physical charms themselves. Though not the elegant beauties that can be found amongst the Daeva, the Roux never the less have a certain earthy charm and they have remarkable endurance. They are also one of the few dynasties happy to engage in blood bonds in order to service elders who require kindred vitae. The matriarch of the brood is Nicolette Roux a kindred of considerable influence in the less salubrious areas of the city.<br /> The <b>Carmichael </b>are a trading and mercantile family, with many connections with the mortal trading community, but do their best to stick to peasant trade and business rather than move into the big leagues in order to avoid the unwanted attention of the nobility who might see this as overstepping their bounds. Despite this, they wield more power than their betters might be comfortable with in the city itself as their networks of merchants and informants are rivalled only by the Fortier. Their patriarch is Louis Carmichael, an elder who claims humble origins but who has a reputation for vicious insight and a fearsome intellect.<br /> The <b>Charron </b>are a semi-nomadic brood whose are skilled at moving overland and visiting places other kindred dare not go. Their reputation as messengers and scouts mean that they have more or less taken over the role that the Gallow\u2019s Post serves in other domains, and as such their influence outside the city is actually considerable, even if within they are seen as a little wild and unpredictable. The Charron supposedly have a patriarch (called simply Charron), but no one has ever met him and information on him has eluded even the great spymasters of the city. <b>The Nosferatu</b><br /><br /> The peasants of the city under the city, the Nosferatu are masters of the tunnels, catacombs, and what few sewers there are. Many Haunts are also working to carve out new tunnels and caverns, with the intention of crafting a great necropolis, and thus escape the rule of the surface. The vast complex is perhaps half-complete, and already many Nosferatu are withdrawing there. Known as the Necropolis Project it has caused much division amongst the clan, as some see it as bordering on treachery and illegality. None of the lines of the Nosferatu are noble, but some are prominent.<br /> The <b>Moreau </b>dominate the criminal underworld of mortal and kindred Paris, and are those to who you turn when you need something illegal done quickly and well. Their ability to give returns of the demands of others has made their skills a valued commodity in the city, and many are willing to pay high prices in order to procure their services. They are led by Phillipe Moreau who is a conniving and entrepreneurial sort, and who is the only patriarch to resist the necropolis project.<br /> The <b>Ventura </b>are a brood of extremely devoted faithful, but who reject the dominance of the Chastain within the Lancea et Sanctum, instead seeing it as their holy duty to return the faith to its humble origins. The majority of its members live in a monastic manner, and its chambers within the necropolis are the deepest, darkest, and dankest. They do, however, harbour some of the most potent users of Theban Sorcery.<br /> The <b>Courtemanche</b> are a small and strange brood whose members are whispered to perform strange experiments, complete odd rituals, and devote themselves to the weirdest and least pleasant parts of the city. They were one of the major driving forces behind the creation of the necropolis, and they have strategically claimed sections of it for themselves. They are also known to have an unusually large number of ghouls in their service, and are led by Gaston, the self-styled Comte Courtemanche (whom the Second Estate have not managed to convince to drop the title, despite their leverage).<br /> The <b>Favager</b> are a young bloodline still not truly come into its own, but swollen with neonates from a rash of embraces. These foul creatures are the &quot;sewer kings&quot; of Paris, a brood of muck-divers and filth-merchants. They not only have control of the sewers but also the befouled rivers and the caverns and tunnels which have been used as dumping grounds. Often filthy and dishevelled, of all kindred in the city they are the lowest - even the other Nosferatu see them as pathetic worms. They are led, if that is the right word, by an ancilla - Pierre Favager. <b>The Minor Clans</b><br /><br /> The vast majority of kindred in the city are drawn from the Ventrue, Daeva, Nosferatu and Gangrel, but there are a small number from other clans.<br /> The <b>Mekhet </b>are too few to really have any sway in the city, and they stand outside of the division between the noble and common lines to some degree. Numbering fewer than five in total, they have a wildly disproportionate amount of power, for they hold the teachings of Auspex close and charge a simply unimaginable amount to access it. There is no bloodline of Mekhet in the city, and its membership in the estates is disparate. You can find two in the First and three in the Third.<br /> The <b>Pijavca</b> are as few as the Mekhet, but are most certainly considered to be peasants by the noble lines of the city. Barely considered kindred at all by the high clans, they escape annihilation by simply being too strange and fearful to attack. These odd putrid swollen blood-maggots are repulsive to most vampires, but they can be a worthy ally to those who take the time to befriend them as they wield strange powers unknown to other kindred. The Pijavca have negotiated an accord with the Nosferatu and have chambers within the necropolis, but they do not count themselves members of the Third Estate. Indeed, they are known to hold no allegiance to anything except each other.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":8}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/33-nifara","name":"Nifara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar33_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"38"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-17T08:21:11-08:00","id":409001,"title":"","content":"<b>The Estates</b><br /><br /> The real division in Paris come in the form of the Estates. Le Requin is the ruler of the city and is considered most prominent, but below him are the Estates. The First Estate, the clergy, is the next most prominent as they are God\u2019s representatives. Below them are the Second Estate, the nobility, who exert the secular power of Le Requin. At the bottom is the Third Estate, the peasantry of Paris, and those who give service to their betters. <b>The First Estate (The Church)</b><br /><br /> As the mortal clergy are ordained by God, so the damned have the ranks of those who are God\u2019s hand upon the earth. Behind dripping candles and fogs of incense smoke these monsters lurk, powerful beyond measure and bathing in the blood of those around them. They sometimes refer to themselves as the <i>Lancea et Sanctum</i>, and certainly they would be considered such by kindred from other cities, but in truth they are their own beasts unique to Paris alone. They are known simply as <i>le clerg\u00e9</i>, or <i>premi\u00e8re \u00e9tat</i>, but they divide themselves further amongst their ranks into <i>\u00e9lev\u00e9</i> (the high clergy) and <i>faible</i> (the low clergy). The high clergy are those drawn from the families of the second estate (noble bloodlines) and only they may rise to the rank of bishop (with one rather notable exception) whilst the low clergy are drawn from the lines of the peasantry and make up the priests, monks, nuns and friars of the city.<br /> Le clerg\u00e9 are the smallest of the estates and have no more than two dozen members of which only a handful are of the high clergy. Despite this they remain hugely powerful and influential, and hundreds of kindred attend their midnight masses with regularity and devotion. They tend to the religious needs of their congregations, devote themselves to the teachings of Longinus, and test the mortal herd as is their duty. Traditional and, one might say, catholic, they are a brutal hard kernel around which the rest of the city forms. Though they have less overt power than the nobility, they have the ear of the Second Estate and of the Prince, and as such cannot be underestimated. The First Estate does not traditionally receive corv\u00e9e as they do not acts a Barons, but instead receive a tithe from the Second Estate as a sign of respect.<br /> The First Estate is led by \u00c9v\u00eaque (Bishop) Chastain who is the formal advisor of the First Estate to the Prince.<br /> <i>Members of the First Estate may learn Theban Sorcery with the storyteller\u2019s approval, though doing so can attract the ire of the Bishop. They are also considered members of the Lancea et Sanctum for the purposes of qualifying for merits, etc.</i> <b>Sects</b><br /><br /> The old guard rule almost supreme in Paris, and as such the members who identify as <b>Catholique</b> dominate the estate. Though many were indeed Roman Catholics in life, the term does not refer to the Church of Holy Rome (which is, of course, superseded by the Temple and Spear in death) but instead uses the word to indicate a continuity of faith and practice, drawing on the rituals and observances of the earliest days of their order. They indeed trace their lineage to Rome, claiming to have established themselves in the city during its time as Lutetia, and their sheer influence makes it believable. All of the high clergy in the city consider themselves catholique, and it is not unpopular amongst the low either.<br /> Some do throw off the shackles of history, though, and declare themselves part of <b>La R\u00e9forme</b>. These individuals still value the teachings and traditions of their faith, but wish to break away from the constraints of the Estates seeing them as an imposition by the secular world on God and his work. The handful of kindred who identify with this faction would seem to be excellent allies of the burgeoning Radical movement but in truth they want to sweep away all secular governance regardless of its type and replace it with an overt theocracy. Despite this they sometimes pander to the underground movement and pamphleteers in order to gain advantage in the city.<br /> The last, and smallest, of the sects found amongst le clerg\u00e9 are <b>Les Protestants</b> who reject the Catholique stance of honouring the religious past completely, and instead advocate adopting a new more puritanical approach to their religious duties. They either were embraced from families which followed mortal protestant faith, or decided to go against the grain after death, finding the pomp and circumstance of the mainstream distasteful. Though few in number Les Protestants continue to survive due to their influence beyond the boundaries of the city, and in particular have a strong relationship with like-minded individuals in Britain and Germany. This means they can sometimes accomplish things that others cannot, and so their positions remain secure for now. Despite this, the idea of any of the high clergy being openly protestant is laughable. <b>The Second Estate (The Nobility)</b><br /><br /> In the ballrooms and dining halls of the City of Light darkness lurks like luxurious velvet, slipping through the cracks into the light of thousands of candles. The mortal nobility feasts and dances and in the shadows lurk their dead compatriots, the Second Estate of the Damned who rule the city with an iron fist. They hold all the land and they receive the corv\u00e9e of their tenants, and in turn pass some of that on to the clergy and Le Requin. Made up of the noble kindred of the city who do not choose to join the clergy, the nobility is the real power in Paris.<br /> Though there is no official \u201cleader\u201d of the Second Estate it is clear to all that Marquis Amaury Rome is much more powerful and influential at court than Comte George Fortier despite the latter\u2019s extensive network of spies and agents. As guardians not only of stability but also the masquerade, the Second Estate prides itself on keep the city peaceful and the peasantry under the domain of those kindred who know better.<br /> <i>Members of the Second Estate are considered members of the Invictus for the purposes of qualifying for merits, etc. In addition, they gain a free dot in one of the following merits of their choice for each dot in Status (Second Estate) they purchase: Resources, Herd, Retainer, Staff.</i> <b>Sects</b><br /><br /> The <b>Cavaliers </b>(or Royalists) make up the overwhelming majority of the Second Estate, to the point where it is barely considering there to be a difference between the two. Royalists give their loyalty to the Prince, and believe that only with the continuation of his rule, and the continuation of the Estates as they stand today can peace be maintained and the masquerade remain unbroken. They see it as their duty to keep the Third Estate in line as they know the commoners are the most likely to overstep the mark and, through poor judgment, make the presence of the kindred known to the populace at large.<br /> The tiny minority of those who do not fall in line with this opinion are sometimes referred to as <b>Compatissant</b>, as they are seen as being sympathetic towards their lesser and thus somewhat soft of heart. They still maintain that the rule of a prince is natural and right, and that the Estates serve a useful purpose, but feel that the harsh rule of the Requin is serving only to risk the masquerade. They believe that by lessening the burden on the peasantry, and giving them more freedom, violence and thus a potential masquerade breach is less likely. <b>The Third Estate (The Peasantry)</b><br /><br /> Made up of the roiling mass of those kindred who are not embraced into the right lines, or who do not feel the call of the church, the Third Estate or <i>troisi\u00e8me</i> <i>\u00e9tat</i> is by far the largest and yet least powerful of the three. Outnumbering the first and second estates five to one at the very least (counting the peasantry isn\u2019t necessarily easy) they are the tenants of the territories of the nobility and the payers of the hated corv\u00e9e. Fractious and prone to infighting, the major reason they have not seized power to date is that getting kindred to look beyond their own interests and work together is a herculean feat. <b>Sects</b><br /><br /> In the streets of Paris the peasantry align themselves into a confusing arrangement of alliances, loyalties and communities, with considerable overlap and lots of partisan hatred. The following are just the major groups not based upon bloodline (for the most part); there are dozens of other tiny short-lived organisations and sects that are too numerous and insignificant to mention here.<br /> The real mover and shaker of the underclasses is of course the <b>Radical</b> movement, who are also known amongst themselves as <b>sang rouge</b>, <b>cartien</b>, <b>r\u00e9volutionnaires</b>, <b>pamphl\u00e9taires</b>, and a dozen other things. A ragtag bunch of discontents, liberals and radicals they are less an organisation and more a tendency. Made up almost exclusively of neonates they are agitators for political change in the same way that their mortal counterparts are. Their goal is to tear down the Prince and his estates, and replace the status quo with a more egalitarian and equal system. What this system would look like is a point of great contention amongst the radicals, and this lack of unity is endemic to all their activities.<br /> Despite their confusing message and lack of structure, the Radical movement is growing at an impressive rate as more and more young kindred become disillusioned with their place in kindred society and yearn for something where they are not forced to serve their barons in such a denigrating manner. Though some are full-time radicals most are members of other sects as well though to date there are no members outside of the Third Estate.<br /> <i>Members of the Radical movement are considered members of the Carthian Movement for the purposes of qualifying for merits, etc. Storytellers may restrict access to Carthian Law if they see fit.</i><br /> Known interchangeably as <b>Bas Rouge</b> and <b>Beauceron</b> (both of which are also names for a breed of particularly tenacious dog known for its loyalty and bulk), it would be more accurate to describe this rough alliance as a tendency akin to the Radicals rather than an organisation in the strictest sense. If the Radicals wish to tear down the estates and the nobility, then the Bas Rouge take the opposite point of view, and work hard to preserve it. In almost all cases this has nothing to do with loyalty or philosophy but rather self-interest, and as such many of the ancilla and elders of the third estate can be found to be members of the Bas Rouge. Many of the third estate are increasingly seeing these traditionalists as traitors, abandoning their own to suffering and misery in favour of their own selfishness, which only heightens the increasing divide between the young and the old in the city.<br /> <i>Members of the Bas Rouge </i><i>are considered members of the Invictus for the purposes of qualifying for merits, etc.</i><br /> <b>La Sous-terre </b>are an important part of everyday life for the dead of Paris as they control the extensive labyrinth of tunnels and chambers that run underneath the city. Verging on apolitical they are willing to barter for almost anything, and they are a reliable source of shelter and sustenance if all else fails you, though such things do not come without a cost. There are a handful of other clans amongst their ranks but the vast majority of La Sous-terre are Nosferatu, and there are whispers of grander structures hollowed out beneath the city, a necropolis only open to the Haunts.<br /> Due to the restrictions on travel for members of the peasantry beyond the reach of their Baron\u2019s territory La Sous-terre act as something of a gatekeeper to wider cooperation amongst the third estate, which acts as another barrier to organisation of the radicals as access to their tunnels is never free. This may be changing, however, as some of the more junior members begin to fall in line with Radical philosophy and supposedly some of them will let their fellows travel for free if they can get away with it.<br /> <i>Members of La Sous-terre may take the Necropolis merit if they wish, regardless of their clan. Nosferatu not of La Sous-terre may not take the Necropolis merit.</i><br /> One of the few respected covenants to find a place amongst the ranks of the peasantry, the <b>Children\u2019s Crusade</b> has a little more leeway than most of the third estate, though for how much longer it is hard to say. Those who are embraced before adulthood in the city are swiftly adopted by the crusade regardless of their allegiance to the estates, though noble kindred have long-since learned not to embrace children for this very reason. Once the covenant was militaristic and feared, but it has been a long time since it held much real power, and these days it is more akin to a support network for those who can never grow up than anything else. Members of the crusade look out for each other, and attacking one usually means attacking them all \u2013 a survival tactic they\u2019ve had to adopt as their reputation is no longer so potent.<br /> All members of the crusade, despite the fact that they no longer march to war, are trained to fight by their peers. Martial prowess is considered an important part of their identity, and internal power squabbles are usually dealt with swiftly and brutally with a show of strength. Scuffles are common amongst the children of the covenant, and other kindred are sometimes surprised or shocked at the bloodiness of their fighting. When a problem plagues the city the crusade is almost always first to answer, though not all problems are solved easily with bloodshed and youthful fury. This can sometimes cause discomfort for those in power, or those who would prefer a more peaceful resolution.<br /> <i>Members of the Children\u2019s Crusade only pay 2xp for dots of the Brawl, Weaponry and Firearms skills, and 1xp for specialties of those skills.</i><br /> <b>L\u2019Acad\u00e9mie du Serpent </b>is one of the most unusual organisations in the city and has a small but influential membership. Based in the Latin Quarter which has become something of a \u201cfree state\u201d (due to a conflict over territory boundaries between two of the nobility) they dominate kindred interaction with the intelligentsia of the city, having a stranglehold over interference with L\u2019Universit\u00e9 de Paris. Its philosophy is complex and in places contradictory but it draws influence primarily from the relatively new ideas put forwards by the Ordo Dracul and the French interpretation of freemasonry.<br /> For L\u2019Acad\u00e9mie the matter of prime import is the perfection of self in service of ultimate truth. They do not seek to throw down or reject God as members of the actual covenant do, but instead hope to commune with what they refer to as the \u201cGodhead\u201d, a concept drawn from hermeticism and freemasonry, through rational inquiry and spiritual development. This heresy makes their existence contentious amongst the Ordo Dracul in a broader sense, who see the adoption of the practices but rejection of the philosophy of the order as an insult to its founder. Despite this no \u201ctrue\u201d Ordo Dracul have managed to gain anything but a tenuous foothold in Paris as they find the political and social constraints of its court to pose a unique difficulty to them, and the uncompromisingly religious attitude of its residents incompatible with their views. As such, the Acad\u00e9mie manages to maintain its independence despite teaching what the dragons see as \u201ctheir\u201d secrets.<br /> The inner circle of L\u2019Acad\u00e9mie operates much like a mortal fraternal organisation, with a hierarchy based upon knowledge and spiritual wisdom and many arcane rituals and a secretive system of \u201chidden truth\u201d. Far closer knit than many groups, a sizable proportion of its members actually live together in a communal haven known as the <i>loge</i> (\u201clodge\u201d) found underneath L\u2019Universit\u00e9 de Paris, where they can study and teach without distraction. Outside of this core membership are the many students and apprentices that L\u2019Acad\u00e9mie takes on, its membership acting as mentors and tutors for kindred drawn from all three estates in return for various kinds of payment. Such tuition ranges from genuine academic teaching such as of the classic or philosophy, to medical and anatomical knowledge and skill, or even mystic education and training in the use of disciplines and devotions. Under no circumstances, however, are students taught the secrets of the Coils, which are reserved for the inner circle alone.<br /> <i>Members of L\u2019Acad\u00e9mie du Serpent may take the Coils of the Dragon with storyteller permission.</i><br /> <b>Notables of Paris</b><br /><br /> There are many important individuals in Paris, and not all of them are kindred. The following are a select few who may be of interest. <b>The High</b><br /><br /> Those kindred who are of noble lines and who rule the city cannot be underestimated for they have the lion\u2019s share of the power and influence that Paris can offer. <b>Henri Rome</b><br /><br /> <i>Le Requin</i><br /> <br /> Henri Rome is in theory the most powerful kindred in the city. Embraced in the mid-16th Century in the city itself, he rose to power less than five decades ago after his grandsire, Adrien Rome, fell into torpor and left the position of Le Requin (\u201cThe Shark\u201d, the equivalent of Prince in other cities) open. He was appointed swiftly and with a minimum of fuss and has a strong level of support amongst the First and Second Estates.<br /> Known for his brutality, his rule has heralded if anything a further increase in the divisive politics of the city, and his approach to troublemakers has been terrifyingly bloody. Where his predecessor was content to follow the will of the First Estate and condemn criminals to torpor, he has pushed hard for executions amongst the peasantry, seeing them as inherently worthless. This approach may in part be due to his isolation from the city proper, as it is known that he keeps his haven at Versailles so as to be near to his preferred herd and his court. In recent years he has even gone so far as to ban any kindred of the Third Estate from setting foot in his favoured town.<br /> How much power Henri actually has is up for some debate as he almost certainly rose to power at the behest of his sire, Amaury Rome, who was generally thought to be next in line of succession. Amaury decided, however, to step aside and allow his childe to take the title and this has caused no end of consternation amongst his political opponents. <b>Jean Baptiste Chastain</b><br /><br /> <i>\u00c9v\u00eaque de Paris</i><br /> <br /> \u00c9v\u00eaque (\u201cBishop\u201d) Jean Baptiste Chastain is the Prince's closest advisor and between the two of them they hold the vast majority of the city's power structure in their grasp. Bishop Chastain claims an improbably long personal history and has publicly spoken of his embrace during the reign of Charlemagne. If true, this would make him by far and away the eldest kindred in the city, and he certainly has a terrifying degree of personal power, which makes the boast considerably more believable.<br /> His views are extremely traditionalist, and his links to the Roman Catholic community in the mortal world make him extremely influential. Those who wish to attract his favour would do well to ensure regular attendance at his masses, and to give plentiful alms to the First Estate. His only unusual piece of policy is that he dissuades his followers of delving into the secrets of Theban Sorcery. There are several explanations for why this would be - publicly he has stated that such devotions are a distraction from the true work of the church, but most whisper that by doing so he leaves himself almost totally unchallenged in terms of mystic power for he himself possesses considerable skill (supposedly learned before his &quot;revelation&quot;).<br /> A shrewd political animal and also a personal powerhouse, Jean Chastain cannot be overestimated. Many have speculated that if he wished he might claim the city for himself, but none would dare to say such in his hearing for he becomes most incensed at the idea of the church taking secular power. <b>Sir Ethan Bertrand Fortier</b><br /><br /> <i>Sh\u00e9rif de Paris</i><br /> <br /> Sir Ethan Bertrand Fortier is the Sh\u00e9rif (\u201csheriff\u201d) of Paris, and yet known more for his wit and social graces than for his strength of arms (relying heavily upon the Sarkozi to provide muscle in cases where it is needed). It is his role to enforce the laws of the city, investigate possible breaches of the masquerade, and bring the perpetrators to justice. In truth, he usually only bothers in establishing guilt when the accused criminal is of the First and Second Estates, and so is more a political tool than a legal one.<br /> That said, he is not to be underestimated: embraced in the early 17th Century he is an experienced kindred, and the head of the Ordre de la Couronne \u00c9pineuse within the city, a knightly order of the Second Estate who hold honour as the highest virtue. They are known for their fearsome duelling abilities, and Sir Ethan is one of their finest swordsmen. <b>George Fortier</b><br /><br /> <i>Le Tisserand</i><br /> <br /> Georges Fortier is the patriarch of his dynasty and is a true monster, albeit a viciously cunning and inhumanly intelligent one. Once he was a notable socialite and womaniser who used his charm and influence to win political favour and defeat his opponents. For decades he was the majordome of the court, tending to its day to day affairs when it was ruled by Adrien Rome, the grandsire of the current Le Requin. From there he established a network of connections, mortal agents, and kindred allies or servants that earned him the nickname Le Tisserand (\u201cThe Weaver\u201d).<br /> When Henri Rome claimed praxis over the city, however, George Fortier and his line more generally saw their support and influence wane. His reputation for being dashing and entertaining soured into one for contrariness and social gaffes, and he quietly withdrew from personal appearances in court so as not to disturb the actions of his brood any more than necessary. Since then he has secluded himself and for the last thirty years or so he has not been seen in public at all. He continues to manipulate the city from shadows, pulling strings to find whatever advantage for his dynasty he can, but he is no more a personally alluring figure. According to rumour he has shed his pretensions of humanity entirely and is now a heaving bloated hulk who consumes both blood and flesh to slake his appetite. <b>Anna Chastain</b><br /><br /> <i>La Langue Ac\u00e9r\u00e9e</i><br /> <br /> One of the most junior members of the Second Estate, Anna Chastain is the grandchilde of Jean Baptiste and a driven and vicious social climber. Though she would never entertain any suggestion that she harbours radical sympathies (she has far too much to lose), she nevertheless rails against the tyranny of her elders and the stiflingly slow accrual of power she has been reduced to. Barely out of her second decade of death she is considered little more than a child by her family who have not deigned yet to release her into kindred society, and yet she is already working to unseat her political rivals and thus ascend the ranks. The fact that such actions are ultimately futile has of course occurred to her, but she is choosing to ignore the fact for her own sanity.<br /> If approached in the right way, Anna might be the only member of the nobility to be prepared to work with self-declared radicals. Though she would be cautious, and of course totally unwilling to do anything she sees as lessening her chance of real power, she\u2019d be more than willing to make alliances of convenience to, say, undermine the power of the Rome family or her grandsire. <b>The Loyalists</b><br /><br /> Though radicalism ferments within the Third Estate, not all kindred are so swayed by fashion and fervour. Many remain loyal to the nobility, clergy and Le Requin, but also the social order that allows for their rule. <b>Evengi Konstantin Ignatov Lestrange</b><br /><br /> <i>L\u2019\u00c9tranger</i><br /> <br /> Evengi Konstantin Ignatov Lestrange is the patriarch of the Lestrange dynasty, and old enough to still claim his original Bulgarian name alongside his French one. Probably the wealthiest kindred in all of France, he maintains a vast network of ghouls and mortal agents throughout the trade routes and commercial centres of the known world, and reaps vast amounts of monies from these ventures. His capital, though not considered important to kindred, is enough to allow his line to put aside the social impediments of their heritage to a certain extent and though they have not risen to the nobility they have prominent places amongst the First Estate.<br /> They are known as prolific moneylenders amongst all the Estates, and do not request monetary repayment, instead asking Laisse of those they lend to. Almost totally amoral, Evengi is content to support any individual who is willing to provide recompense, a mercenary bent which does not endear him greatly to the Prince... but who is unable to do anything about it on the grounds that the Rome family is severely indebted to the Lestrange. The patriarch is known as L\u2019\u00c9tranger (\u201cThe Stranger\u201d) to those not of his family, as he still has the reputation of being an outsider. <b>Maurice Sarkozi</b><br /><br /> <i>Le Petit Chien</i><br /> <br /> Maurice Sarkozi is the patriarch of the Sarkozi family, the enforcers and muscles of the nobility and in particular of the Rome family and Le Requin. Embraced in the late 15th Century, Maurice is one of the eldest kindred in the city, and extremely pleased with his position and that of his brood. Vastly personally powerful, he is a master of all three physical disciplines, and has perfected a number of unique devotions that allow him to further enhance his monstrous form granted by Protean.<br /> One famous incident concerned a group of Khaibit assassins who had travelled to Paris to slay Amaury Rome: Maurice attacked all nine of the assailants at once, and gave each of them final death within a minute. His loyalty borders on fanatical and he is the unofficial leader of the Bas Rouge.<br /> His opponents mock his servile attitude to Le Requin by calling him Le Petit Chien (\u201cThe Little Dog\u201d), but Sarkozi mocks them in turn by adopting it as a badge of pride. He often refers to himself in this manner, though for others to do so in his hearing would be\u2026 unwise. <b>Jean Moreau</b><br /><br /> <i>L'Ex\u00e9cuteur</i><br /> <br /> One of the sprawling Moreau brood, like his siblings Jean is a man whose history is steeping in criminality and violence. Hulking and not that bright he acts as an enforcer for the family, taking care of overt threats to their dominance and breaking bones to make a point when he needs to. As his mortal life was remarkably similar it did not take Jean much time to become accustomed to his new place in the world. He attends mass with fierce loyalty and is extremely pious, the teachings of Longinus having a natural lure to his temperament. He is considered extremely dependable, and though Philippe Moreau would never trust him with anything too complicated, Jean can be relied upon to follow through with a task and get results. <b>The Radicals</b><br /><br /> Living on the fringes of acceptable society, the following ragtag bunch are those who wish to see the Estates brought low and freedom given to all kindred. <b>Gaston Courtemanche</b><br /><br /> <i>Le Comte Folle</i><br /> <br /> \u201cComte\u201d Gaston Courtemanche is a brash and uncompromising figure, who has a certain dark glamour about him which attracts much attention. He has claimed the \u201cfree territory\u201d of the Latin Quarter as it falls between the baronies of Comtesse Giselle Rome and Baron Urbain Fortier who have been in a row over whose predecessors were in a longstanding row over whose territory L\u2019Universit\u00e9 de Paris, and now are in the situation of rather strongly not wanting it in order to not have to deal with the Courtemanche family, a tiny brood entirely made up of Gaston\u2019s childe and all mmebers of L\u2019Acad\u00e9mie du Serpent who are known for being difficult and intractable. As such it remains an odd aberrance in the social structure of the city where a number of individuals reside without owing corv\u00e9e to anyone.<br /> Stepping into the gap left by the nobility the self-style Comte Courtemanche (known less respectfully by many as Le Comte Folle or \u201cThe Crazed Count\u201d) has set himself up as a sort of informal liege lord in the tiny district. As he is also one of the more prominent members of L\u2019Acad\u00e9mie du Serpent he is well-placed to keep his tiny group of followers in line due the fact that he lives with most of them. More wild academic than nobleman, he has a slightly strange reputation for being extremely charismatic and compelling though those who encounter this side of him often struggle to explain their impressions after the fact.<br /> For now the Comte is tolerated though if he continues to attempt to attend Le Requin\u2019s court at Versailles this may not be true for long. Whether this is all part of some cunning political game, or he is secretly harbouring radical sympathies, or whether he actually is just insane no one is entirely sure. <b>Louis Carmichael</b><br /><br /> <i>Le Boucher</i><br /> <br /> Louis Carmichael is the patriarch of the Carmichael family, and probably the most accessible and down to earth of all the heads of the city's families. Louis like to brag about his childhood as a butcher in Paris in the 16th Century, and still claims that he is as humble as he ever was as a mortal. He is a frequent attendant of the parlours and cafes of the city, and is free with his opinions... though said opinions are often conciliatory and placating rather than radical and revolutionary. Louis is whispered to have darker connections with the kine as well, and his nickname as Le Boucher (\u201cThe Butcher\u201d) is both metaphorical and literal. <b>Abb\u00e9 Adolfo Ventura</b><br /><br /> <i>L'abb\u00e9 Silencieuse</i><br /> <br /> Abb\u00e9 Adolfo Ventura is a pious kindred who believes deeply in the teachings of the Sanctified Church but who rejects the form that it takes in the city. His brood are less a family and more a monastic order, working together in the service of God. Adolfo himself took a vow of silence two decades ago, and now only communicates via the discipline of Auspex, which was taught to him by one of his earliest followers (one of the few Mekhet in the city). He is also an extremely proficient practitioner of Theban Sorcery and a renowned occult expert. He is also one of the few kindred in the city to have encountered, and survived, a Strix, and was responsible for driving the creature from the city more than a century ago.<br /> His isolationist stance has made him deeply unpopular amongst the high clergy, and though he remains officially outside of the Third Estate he and his small family are treated rather more like peasants than priests. In particular his flouting of the Bishop\u2019s rules on the learning of Theban Sorcery have made him something of a pariah. <b>Period Firearms</b><br /><br /> The ascension of the gun as the main weapon of war has totally changed violence, but firearms in the late 18<sup>th</sup> century are not without their drawbacks. Almost all personal firearms of this period are muzzle loaded (the powder, wadding and ball being inserted down the barrel ready for use) and flintlock (which produces a spark that ignites the powder when the trigger is pulled). This combination makes firearms both slow to reload (most soldiers would be able to fire 2 or 3 times per minutes) and prone to misfires and other accidents (such as premature firing, powder explosions and so on). Despite this firearms are used extensively as they cannot be rivalled for sheer stopping power. <b>Reloading</b><br /><br /> How long it takes you to reload a muzzle-loaded firearm depends on you skill and experience. Reloading always requires an Intelligence + Firearms check. A failure indicates the packing was bad and you must start over. A dramatic failure usually indicates a misfire, powder explosion, or other serious issue \u2013 the storyteller should decide the outcome. Reloading require and instant action each round during the entire reloading process. If you stop reloading during this process then you must start all over again from scratch when you resume.<br /> An untrained marksman (0 dots in Firearms) will struggle to load the weapon at all. It will take them a full minute (20 instant actions). With 1 or 2 dots of Firearms a character can reload in 30 seconds (10 instant actions). With 3 dots of Firearms the character can reload in even less time \u2013 7 instant actions, or just over 20 seconds. With 4 dots of Firearms the character can reload very fast indeed, in about 15 seconds (5 instant actions). With 5 dots they are incredibly capable, and can reload in just less than 10 seconds (3 instant actions). <b>Misfires and Other Problems</b><br /><br /> Whenever a flintlock weapon is fired, it might misfire \u2013 on a dramatic failure the powder reserve explodes early, catches fire, splits the barrel or the weapon otherwise misbehaves badly. Such misfires almost always damage the weapon or at least mean it requires cleaning before using again, and can sometimes injure the user.<br /> As a flintlock weapon is used black powder builds up in the barrel slowly clogging it and making it less accurate and also more prone to misfires. For every shot made with the weapon all Firearms checks using the weapon (including reloading) suffer a -1 penalty. For rifles this penalty rises to -2 per shot, as the rifling is easily clogged. This penalty is completely removed when the player spends some time cleaning the weapon, but otherwise continues to accrue regardless of how much time you leave between shots.<br /> Flintlocks also unleash a flurry of sparks when fired. For most this is just distracting and occasionally results in a few minor burns to the hands and face, but for kindred this can be terrifying. The storyteller should require a frenzy check as appropriate when firing a flintlock.<br /> Flintlocks also create clouds of thick smoke when fired. For the round they are fired and the round after this acts as a barrier to visual acuity, inflicting a -1 penalty on all appropriate checks. This penalty can be cumulative if multiple weapons are fired, or if weapons are fired on each consecutive round.<br /> It is also possible to pack and fire a flintlock so that instead of firing a ball it spews flaming black powder. You must choose to set up a firearm to fire in this way when you load it. Its ranges become 3/6/12 and its damage remains the same, but the same it inflicts is in the form of a small fireball. This might set targets on fire or have other effects (like on vampires). <b>Weapon and Armour Statistics</b><br /><br /> Use the melee weapons and archaic armour chart from Requiem 2<sup>nd</sup> Edition, and the firearms statistics below: <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table align=\"center\" class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\" style=\"border-spacing: 0px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Damage</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Ranges</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Initiative</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Strength</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Size</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Availability</b></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Pistol, pocket</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">2</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">5/10/20</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">-1</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">2</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">1</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">\u2022\u2022</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Pistol, coat</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">3</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">10/20/40</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">-2</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">3</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">1</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">\u2022\u2022</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Pistol, horse</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">4</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">10/20/40</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">-3</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">3</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">2</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">\u2022\u2022\u2022</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Musket</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">4</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">25/50/100</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">-4</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">2</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">3</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">\u2022\u2022\u2022</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Rifle</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">4</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">50/100/200</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">-5</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">2</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">3</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022</td> </tr> </table></div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/33-nifara","name":"Nifara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar33_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"38"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-17T08:23:04-08:00","id":409002,"title":"","content":"Apparently the PDF did not attach....<br /> <br /> A link for Google drive:<a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B-ssoH7YZYcYZUhKUE5VMTV3ams/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B-s...ew?usp=sharing</a><br /> Here's a link on onedrive instead: <a href=\"http://1drv.ms/1yRYm1l\" target=\"_blank\">http://1drv.ms/1yRYm1l</a><br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/33-nifara","name":"Nifara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar33_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"38"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-17T12:58:19-08:00","id":409240,"title":"","content":"Wow, very cool stuff! I'm still in the process of reading it, but I do think it's a great idea and you've done a great job assembling the details together, bravo!<br /> <br /> Have you run anything in this setting, or is it fresh off the imagination press? It's very cool.<br /> <br /> I think it's a nice touch that they hew more closely to the mortal conception of the Estates as a thing that sets Paris apart from other cities. I think I'd like to see a bit more about what makes the First Estate different from the regular Sanctified, as one thing. I get that they're the distinctive form that the Lancea takes in Paris, but having a little bit more information to set them apart would really help characterize them.<br /> <br /> I also think it's worth noting that the Devs have gone on record (we see it a bit in the published settings) about how the Sanctified Faith really only resembles traditional Christianity in terms of aesthetics. When we talk about the Protestants and La Reforme versus the Catholique, do we have any idea of why they chose specifically to identify with these monikers shared by mortal movements? I think conceptually they're fine, but I might suggest giving the Protestants and the Catholiques different, more peculiar names. So like, we might call Les Protestants something like... The Austere, or a fuller name referring to their more spartan view of Sanctified doctrine.<br /> <br /> Those are just some suggestions though. I'll definitely add more comments as I read more.<br /> <br /> EDIT: I like what I read! The Flintlocks are a cool bit at the end.<br /> <br /> Out of curiousity, what made you go with the names for the lineages of Kindred? I like that element of the setting and the details you offer there. One point of clarification that might be worth making is distinguishing the Bloodlines from the Lineages. While these two words mean the same thing normally, Bloodlines carry a connotation of greater changes to the Blood, while Lineages can be baseline Clans without any special powers. The Lapointe are a Bloodline for instance, while the Chastain seem to be a noble lineage.<br /> <br /> Lastly, if you felt so inclined, would you be willing to share your Inspirations? I found <i>Requiem for Regina's </i>section incredible helpful for looking stuff up, and for the excellent reference that is the Time Traveler's Guide to Elizabethan England. If you know any resources that might also help a storyteller bring the setting to life, sharing those would be really appreciated.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":159,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","user":"Leetsepeak","date":". 02-18-2015, 12:28 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-18T02:41:42-08:00","id":409839,"title":"","content":"Thanks for the positive feedback Leetsepeak!<br /> <br /> I ran a short game last year (about 10 sessions) based in the setting, but it's been tweaked and updated since then to bring it more in line with Dark Eras, but also to smooth out some of the rough edges of the setting we encountered. I have a new game set in this version starting next week, however, and I now have character sheets from everyone and lots of enthusiasm. I'll let you know how it goes when we start playing - the pitch is that they are a group of radicals looking to overthrow the court.<br /> <br /> The Estates for me were one of the most important things of the setting, but I think you're right that I didn't really give them enough of a word count. I'll definitely be adding to their descriptions and giving some more detail, but I think I'll leave it a few weeks until I see how they work in play before writing more.<br /> <br /> The whole Catholic/Protestant things is a bit tricky really. On the one hand I'm aware that the Devs have taken the position that the Sanctified Faith doesn't really have much in common with Christianity, but in a city like Paris where mortal religion is so important (it's not been that long since the French Wars of Religion, and Protestants are still treated extremely poorly under law) I felt i was important to mirror the mortal division. It's particularly of note because of the historic ties between protestants and radicals, and I felt that this was good fodder for sects within the First Estate. The other thing I tried to get to come across (but potentially it needs more work) is that the Catholic sect of the First Estate is Catholic in the literal sense, rather than in the sense of the Roman Catholic church - they claim a continuous history of teaching and ritual stemming from the earliest days of the Longinian church. Again this probably needs more work, and it's well worth pointing out. I'll have a serious think about how I want to handle that.<br /> <br /> I'm glad you like the Flintlocks! I feel we need more good historical rules.<br /> <br /> The lineage names were chosen usually, and this is kind of bad to admit, because they are puns! Many of the lines were named thematically, with the intention of having the name reinforce some of there common roles or ideals (the Lapointe, for example, comes from the French word for Lance, as they will potentially &quot;lance the boil&quot; of corruption in the city in their role of having seen things from both sides). Some of the others were picked because I felt they were solid French names that resonated well with the setting, but were ultimately arbitrary.<br /> <br /> In some ways the confusion over line, lineage, dynasty, brood and bloodline are totally intentional. The original concept of the setting was to illustrate how different kindred groups could be within clans, and bloodlines are an important part of that. I also wanted to leave it open to some interpretation as to how many bloodlines there actually are hanging around in the city - some groups may want more, whilst other might not want the hassle of extra mechanics. Later on I'll dig out some of the custom bloodline mechanics I've written for the setting and post them up.<br /> <br /> I'll try and narrow down my inspirations into a more palatable list later - watch this space.<br /> <br /> --Edit--<br /> <br /> As promised a list of inspirations:<br /> <b>Les Liasions Dangereuses (Pierre Choderlos de Laclos):</b> This book deserves a spot on this list not only for its fascinating insight into the corruption of the French aristocracy, but also because it is thought to be a factor in the revolution beginning at all. Both compelling and fascinating, and a beautiful idea of what the Second Estate might be like in practice.<br /> <b>Crime and punishment (Fyodor Dostoyevsky):</b> Though it's set in St Petersburg almost half a century after the revolution, it's hard to find a better expression of the horrific claustrophobia of poverty, injustice and the terrible weight of the aristocracy upon the poor. Raid it for wonderful descriptions of the foetid streets that evoke La Rive Gauche.<br /> <b>Les Miserable (Victor Hugo):</b> Set in the right place this time but 40 years too late this tale of the 1830s uprising in Paris and one man's struggle for redemption in a world where the poor and downtrodden still have no voice is a bitter vision of the fallout of the French Revolution. If nothing else the descriptions of the actions of the revolutionaries in Paris will give you lots of ideas, and the feel of the novel is unparalleled.<br /> <b>The Rights of Man (Thomas Paine):</b> Though not penned until 1790 this vicious work of political philosophy remained and important part of the French Revolution for years, and was a rallying cry for those who felt that a Republic was the only system of government that was acceptable. If only to gain some insight into the kind of works that the Radicals might embrace, it's a great resource.<br /> <b>The French Revolution (Thomas Carlyle):</b> The definitive volume on the Revolution and written only 50 years afterwards, this book is a fascinating insight into the history and motivations of revolution in general, and the French Revolution in particular.<br /> <b>Assassin's Creed Unity (Ubisoft):</b> Who cares what it's like as a game? A simulation of the city of Paris in 1:1 ratio based on thousands of hours of research that matches up almost perfectly to the maps of the time. It's a fantastic experience if only to wander around and sightsee.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":33,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/33-nifara","user":"Nifara","date":". 02-18-2015, 07:31 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/33-nifara","name":"Nifara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar33_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"38"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-18T06:43:16-08:00","id":410041,"title":"","content":"And again as promised, some Bloodline workups. They're a little sparse in background material (they're all being made for the players in my game and we've decided to work out some of their history in game) but they're mechanically sound.<br /> <br /> <b>The Fergris</b><br /> <br /> The Fergris are a bloodline with strong connections to both the French military and to northern Africa. Founded in the early 1680s by one of the first French kindred to travel to the colonies in Algeria, it has roots in the iron-rich country that was his adoptive home. Rumoured to have been a man known as Louis Villetrous, tales within the bloodline suggest that their founder had been a military engineer and sapper who made connections with some of the native kindred of the region, learning from their mystic insights to gain a better understanding of his own vitae and the structures he dealt with every day. He achieved a transformation of that vitae whilst still an ancilla, an unusual feat, and his inexperience might explain the somewhat unpredictable results that arose.<br /> <b>Parent Clan:</b> Gangrel<br /> <b>Disciplines:</b> Auspex, Celerity, Protean, Resilience<br /> <b>Weakness:</b> The Fergris, upon entry into the bloodline, find their skin and flesh to become pale and grey almost as if they were kindred of much greater age. In truth this cosmetic change is due to unusually high concentrations of iron in their blood. Their real weakness, however, related to that blood\u2019s unique properties \u2013 attempts to feed others their blood (whether mortal or vampire) results in horrific injury and pain to the subject as the vitae solidifies and crystallises whilst in their throat and stomach. Anyone who drinks Fergris vitae suffers a point of aggravated damage for each point of vitae ingested, their body wracked with pain and convulsions as their digestive system desperately struggles to cope. Whilst most can survive the process it is not subtle, and symptoms involve vomiting blood, intense stomach pain, fever and hallucinations.<br /> <b>Gift:</b> Known amongst the bloodline as the durcissement, Fergris vitae has an extremely unusual property: upon leaving the body it hardens within a minute or so into raw and rough iron, nearly indistinguishable from the metal apart from the deep reddish hue that makes it look rusted. The bloodline has found many uses for this property of their blood.<ul><li>When a Fergris spills a point or more of Vitae (usually by cutting upon the body) the vitae will harden into solid iron after roughly a minute and will not disintegrate until exposed to either fire or sunlight, the touch of which will turn it to ash in short order.</li> <li>Soaking clothing in their blood allows a member of the bloodline to turn it into crude armour. It takes 1 vitae to produce a single point of armour for a single location through this method, making it expensive but useful in a desperate situation.</li> <li>When struck by a weapon that breaks the skin and bites into the flesh of the Fergris they can reflexively spend one or more points of vitae to let their blood harden around the weapon. This traps it within the body and it can only be removed with a successful Strength + Stamina check with a penalty equal to the number of points of vitae spent beyond the first. When the weapon is removed it inflicts a point of bashing on the vampire. If left in the body and not removed it will fall out during the kindred\u2019s next daysleep.</li> <li>Almost any other application of this strange hardening property is available to the inventive kindred, but the storyteller is within their rights to require additional vitae expenditure and/or appropriate checks if they rule it is reasonable in the first place.</li> </ul><b>Le Garde du Sang</b><br /> <br /> Le Garde du Sang are a bloodline of martial and mercenary kindred with a chequered past and a poor reputation amongst members of the Invictus in particular. Founded in the 1740s by Am\u00e9lie Dusand a former Invictus Knight who was ejected from her order after the political intrigues of one of the senior members of her covenant left her as a useful distraction. Furious at her ignoble treatment and swearing that she would never betray anyone else for personal gain in the same way that her lords had betrayed her, she devoted herself to acting on the behalf of the downtrodden and disenfranchised kindred of Europe. Travelling from city to city she made a name for herself as a mercenary to be trusted, and one that was not available for hire by those who ruled a domain. She attracted a number of acolytes and inducted them into the bloodline she had developed to ensure trust and honour amongst those who served the underclasses.<br /> <b>Parent Clan:</b> Daeva<br /> <b>Disciplines:</b> Animalism, Celerity, Majesty, Vigor<br /> <b>Weakness:</b> Le Garde du Sang pride themselves on their skill and honour, but they cannot handle even the faintest whiff of betrayal. If they are betrayed this is an immediate trigger to frenzy. In addition, if a kindred with the Dominant Condition makes <i>any action that acts against the character or their interests</i> then this is also a trigger for frenzy.<br /> <b>Gift:</b> Known as <i>supplier</i> (\u201csupplicate\u201d) by Le Garde du Sange, their unique ability is a form of blood oath that is enforced by their very beast.<ul><li>A member of Le Garde du Sange gains a new aspect of the beast: The Servile Beast. Using the Servile Beast works just the same as any of the standard Bestial Triad.</li> <li>The Servile Beast is Beast-As-Follower. It needs an alpha to follow, a master to obey. It struggles to enforce its own will on others, instead seizing upon the will of its betters, and giving into their commands. It wants to help those it serves, and better then through its actions. It evokes the need for leadership, and causes the Dominant Condition.</li> <li>Use all the standard rules for predatory aura except that if the kindred is using Servile Beast as the aggressor and their target chooses Flight the <i>supplicant </i>must make a convenient excuse or escape to pull away, and it is the supplicant who looks inferior or foolish.</li> </ul><b>C</b><b>ondition: Dominant</b><br /> The character is infused with a feeling of power and success over the supplicant (the vampire who imposed this situation), and you instantly become reliant upon them and wish to defend your new servant. You get a -2 die penalty to any rolls you make when the supplicant is not present. In addition you suffer all the effects First Stage blood bond with the supplicant (they count as your regnant) and you will therefore intrinsically trust them more than you otherwise would. This is not a true blood bond and ends when this condition ends and is not subject to any of the other rules concerning blood bonds.<br /> This Condition fades naturally after a number of nights equal to the Blood Potency of the vampire who caused it (the supplicant).<br /> After resolving Dominant, you character cannot be subject to this Condition again for a full month.<br /> <b>Possible Sources:</b> The Servile Beast<br /> <b>Resolution:</b> You are betrayed or harmed by the supplicant.<br /> <b>Beat:</b> n/a<br /> <br /> <b>The Lapointe</b><br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\"> The Lapointe dynasty spreads its influence in less direct ways than many others, but its chians once forged are like iron. They are specialists in using the blood bond to control others, making them into soldiers for their armies. However, they are cunning, for the blood bonds they create do not bind their thralls to them directly, but rather to their cause.<br /> <b>Weakness:</b> The Lapointe's unique blood means they are more susceptible to the blood bonds of others. You cannot resist a blood bond, even by spending Willpower.<br /> </span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\"><b>Gift:</b> The Lapointe's blood is particularly invigorating and compelling, and has a strange and potent effect on those who consume it.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\"><ul><li>When an individual would normally suffer a blood bond to a member of the Lapointe, they instead suffer it to a particular goal or ideal determined by the Lapointe. The Lapointe must specify this ideal at the time the blood bond is first created, though if a bond fades and is remade, they can choose differently the next time. It could be something as general as &quot;Justice&quot; or as specific as &quot;the slaying of Bishop Marcus&quot;. These bonds, other than the changes noted below, operate exactly as normal bonds do.</li> <li>The First Stage blood bond now represents excitement and enthusiasm for the chosen ideal. It doesn't matter what they normally want, suddenly their focus shifts. When <i>anyone</i> takes a Social maneuver against the thrall where the end goal is to further that ideal, cause, or concept, their impression is considered one step higher. In addition, they gain a +1 die bonus on all Social actions and Discipline rolls against them, assuming against the end goal is in service of the ideal. Causing direct or indirect harm to the ideal or cause constitutes a breaking point at Humanity 5/Integrity-2</li> <li>The Second Stage blood bond now represents obsession and devotion to the chosen idea. Not only is it exciting, its fundamentally a good idea - it becomes and important facet of their belief in the world around them. The impression of social maneuvers (as above) is now considered <i>two</i> steps higher, and the bonus increases to +2. Causing harm to the ideal or cause is now a breaking point at Humanity 3 or Integrity on a -3.</li> <li>The Third Stage blood bond is simple and absolute zealotry, a totally consuming and obsessive need to further the cause. The impression on social maneuvers is always considered perfect, and the bonus rises to +3. Causing harm is now at Humanity 1/Integrity -5. Finally, all Discipline effects applied to the target that further the cause gain additional duration - scene becomes night, night becomes week, week becomes month, month becomes year. At the third stage it shatters all other bonds (even normal ones) and prevents further bonds from forming.</li> </ul></span></span><br /> <br /> For some reason I got very hung up on the Lapointe, and ended up writing a whole bunch of different version of their bloodline. Here are the other versions I worked up but ultimately aren't getting used (I used some of the ideas from the last option in Le Garde du Sang instead):<br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">The Lapointe dynasty has made a name for itself by being superb spymasters and secret-keepers. Learning from their cousins the Nosferatu the skills of subtlety, they combine it with their raw social power to extra hidden information in a variety of ways.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\"><b>Weakness:</b> The Lapointe are almost paranoid about revealing secrets, even to each other, and each one ends up with a treasure-trove of hidden information. Revealing any secret where it is not <i>absolutely essential</i> to do so requires them to overcome their own instincts and blood, and constitutes a Breaking Point at a level determined by the Storyteller (based upon how trivial the reveal is).</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\"><b>Gift:</b> The Lapointe have developed the arts known as Cach\u00e9 which play upon their ability to uncover and keep secrets, as well as their natural inclinations towards the arts of Obfuscate and Majesty. All Lapointe gain the first devotion (\u00e9nigme) upon joining the bloodline, and can purchase any of the others for 2 experiences each (e<i>dit: I will write more devotions if this is the variation we pick)</i></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\"><b>Enigme</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">The most basic gift of the Lapointe is able to hide something so thoroughly that they themselves do not know it until a specific circumstance triggers the release of memory. This ensures that a secret remains a secret, no matter the powers brought to bear against the individual.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">By focusing on a particular piece of information or memory the Lapointe can spend a point of Vitae and conceal it even from himself. A memory of this kind cannot be accessed by the kindred themselves, or read using any form of discipline or sorcery, whether the mind-reading of Auspex, the commands of Dominate, or the divination of Cruac. When you activate this power, specify a trigger for the memory to be released - a particular circumstance, event, or period of elapsed time. When this occurs, the memory returns, and can be accessed as normal. You can choose to add no trigger - in which case the memory is simply eliminate forever. You cannot use this power to &quot;undo&quot; breaking points, or regain Humanity.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\"><b>Faire Courir</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">With this power the Lapointe can cause a secret to become extremely public knowledge in a terrifyingly short period of time. The Lapointereveals a secret to another (which may be a breaking point as normal) and then spends a point of Vitae to add the power of his blood to his words. The individual thus spoken to gains the <i>Gossipy </i>Condition (see below). Prerequisite: Majesty 2</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\"><b>Bavard</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">Some secrets are painful to hear, and this gift allows the Lapointe to give them even greater edge. You can use this power when you reveal a secret to someone who is personally hurt by it, spending a Vitae point to unleash the secret in such a way that it seems to scream out to them in fury. They, and any others who hear it and who are upset or hurt by it, must immediately make a Frenzy check at a penalty equal to the Lapointe's Blood Potency. If they fall into Frenzy, then the Beast's (first) desire is to harm those who have hear the secret<i>other</i> than the Lapointe.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\"><b>Condition: Gossipy</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">The character is consumed with the need to tell others about a secret they have uncovered, whether or not they would normally engage in such things. Whenever they are in a calm and civilised conversation (not a shouting match) with another they <i>must</i> divulge the secret which is the focus of this Condition unless they spend a Willpower point to avoid doing so. If they do tell them the secret, then they are now subject to this Condition.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">This condition fades at the next dawn or dusk after receiving it.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\"><b>Possible Sources:</b> Cach\u00e9, really juicy gossip</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\"><b>Resolution:</b> The character tells two other individuals the secret.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\"><b>Beat:</b> n/a</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\"> The Lapointe are spymasters beyond all compare, and their ghouls are at the heart of their manipulations. They create vast numbers of them, and they serve as the backbone of their spy networks, serving the whole bloodline equally.<br /> <b>Weakness:</b> The Lapointe create ghouls of exceptional power and impressive loyalty, but this devotion to their development is at the cost of their other powers of their blood. Consuming their blood does not cause a blood bond to form in kindred (though it does in mortal as normal). This weakness is a closely guarded secret of the bloodline, as they know it would make them very attractive to Elders as a food supply. They can suffer from a blood bond just as other kindred.<br /> <br /> <b>Gift:</b> The Lapointe gift is known as Soul\u00e8vement, and allows them to create ghouls with greater ease than most kindred, control them with less effort, and to grant them impressive gifts.<br /> </span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\"><ul><li>The Lapointe can create groups of ghouls at once in a ceremony they know as the Se Rassasier. They do this by spilling a number of Vitae into a vessel equal to the number of mortals to become ghouls, and then investing a single Willpower. Those who drink from the vessel become ghouls. All who are to become ghouls must be present when Se Rassasier is performed, and the Vitae shed into the vessel become inert at the end of the scene.The ghouls created in such a manner all gain a Vitae as normal, but none receive the Willpower.</li> <li>The ghouls created by the Lapointe are shared amongst the family line. Blood bonds within ghouls created through Se Rassasier by a member of the Lapointe apply to all members of the bloodline as if they were <i>all</i> the regnant.</li> <li>Ghouls created by the Lapointe through Se Rassasier are uniquely powered. They receive a single dot of each of the in-clan disciplines of the Lapointe at creation (Majesty, Vigor, Celerity, and Obfuscate) instead of the normal two discipline dots of their choice.</li> <li>Ghouls created through the Se Rassasier possess kindred senses as if they possessed Blood Potency equal to their regnant's value.</li> </ul></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">The Advocatus Diaboli is the Gift of the Lapointe, and revolves around the need to provide an opposing view, whether to ensure the path chosen is correct, or to find a different way forwards.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\"><ul><li>The Lapointe have a fourth aspect of the beast to call upon apart from the standard Bestial Triad - the Rebellious Beast. This is the Beast-As-Reactionary. Its every instinct is to reject the status quo, and to combat authority. It inspires the need to react against assumptions, reject the safe course of action, and to rebel against authority. It evokes the primal needs to assert individuality in its victims, and causes the Rebellious Condition (below).</li> <li>When a member of the Lapointe chooses to fight another kindred who is Lashing Out, and they choose the Rebellious Beast to do so, they have the upper hand, as it is the nature of that aspect to fight authority. They need not spend a Willpower, even if they are of a lower Blood Potency, and on a draw they gain the +2 bonus in addition to imposing their condition on their opponent (even if they also suffer a condition).</li> <li>When a Lapointe engages a social maneuvering attempt, they find the most challenging opponents the most engaging and invigorating. When they succeed in such a challenge against an opponent who has as more doors than their Blood Potency, they gain a Blood Beat.</li> </ul><b>Condition: Rebellious</b><br /> </span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">Your character must undermine authority and assert her individuality at all costs. She cannot accept the status quo easily or readily, and suffers for doing so. Any time she performs an action which directly enforces or upholds the authority of another (not herself) she suffers a -2 die penalty on any rolls. This includes contested and extended rolls. As well, any rolls to tempt or coerce her into rebellious or individualistic action achieve exceptional success on three successes instead of five.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">This Condition fades (does not resolve) naturally after a number of nights equal to the Blood Potency of the vampire who caused it. In the case of the predatory aura, this is the vampire who won the conflict. In the case of testing for detachment, this is the vampire's own Blood Potency.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">After resolving Rebellious, your character cannot be subject to this Condition again for a full month. Whilst suffer from it, if kindred, she possesses the Rebellious Beast aspect in addition to the standard three, and is forced to use it when fighting another kindred who is Lashing Out. She does not gain the bloodline benefits on this check, however, nor any other benefits of the bloodline's gift.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\"><b>Possible Sources:</b> A rebellious predatory aura conflict, facing a breaking point.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\"><b>Resolution:</b> Perform an action which directly acts against authority and which constitutes a breaking point.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\"><b>Beat:</b> n/a</span></span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/33-nifara","name":"Nifara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar33_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"38"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-18T10:01:33-08:00","id":410263,"title":"","content":"I like these! Going through them still.<br /> <br /> On the Fergris, I suggest moving the bulk of the flavor text for the weakness into the Gift, because it actually grants them an incredible means of preventing anybody from feeding on them, in addition to an easy means of causing aggravated damage in people by force-feeding them Vitae. I'd maybe lower it to Lethal for Kindred and Aggravated for humans. Rationale: Kindred suffer Lethal damage from gross bodily harm, and tearing up their insides with hard iron metal-blood seems to be in that arena. For humans however, this effect would be absolutely <i>ruinous</i> and likely to kill them from the internal damage they suffer. I bet it wouldn't be a quick death, either. Yikes.<br /> <br /> What I'm hearing as the <i>actual </i>weakness is that the Fergris can't really blood-bond people without killing them or horribly mutilating their innards. I'd focus the flavor text around that element, because the current focus is more of a boon than a bane. (Incidentally, cannot blood bond has a lot of precedence in first and 2nd edition as a bloodline bane, though the punishing nature of the Fergris's inability to safely do so is pretty unique.) I like the idea though, it occupies a similar conceptual space as the Norvegi, but approaches it in a different and unique manner. Nice!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":159,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","user":"Leetsepeak","date":". 02-18-2015, 01:05 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-20T02:12:17-08:00","id":412571,"title":"","content":"I've reworded the bloodline weakness of the Fergris as per your suggestion - I agree, it muddies the waters to not have the actual weakness spelt out more clearly.<br /> <br /> I've also added some more information about the First Estate in the city as per your suggestion, which you can find below:<br /> <b>The First Estate (The Church)</b><br /><br /> As the mortal clergy are ordained by God, so the damned have the ranks of those who are God\u2019s hand upon the earth. Behind dripping candles and fogs of incense smoke these monsters lurk, powerful beyond measure and bathing in the blood of those around them. They sometimes refer to themselves as the <i>Lancea et Sanctum</i>, and certainly they would be considered such by kindred from other cities, but in truth they are their own beasts unique to Paris alone. They are known simply as <i>le clerg\u00e9</i>, or <i>premi\u00e8re \u00e9tat</i>, but they divide themselves further amongst their ranks into <i>\u00e9lev\u00e9</i> (the high clergy) and <i>faible</i> (the low clergy). The high clergy are those drawn from the families of the second estate (noble bloodlines) and only they may rise to the rank of bishop (with one rather notable exception) whilst the low clergy are drawn from the lines of the peasantry and make up the priests, monks, nuns and friars of the city.<br /> <br /> Le clerg\u00e9 are the smallest of the estates and have no more than two dozen members of which only a handful are of the high clergy. Despite this they remain hugely powerful and influential, and hundreds of kindred attend their midnight masses with regularity and devotion. They tend to the religious needs of their congregations, devote themselves to the teachings of Longinus, and test the mortal herd as is their duty. Traditional and, one might say, catholic, they are a brutal hard kernel around which the rest of the city forms. Though they have less overt power than the nobility, they have the ear of the Second Estate and of the Prince, and as such cannot be underestimated. The First Estate does not traditionally receive corv\u00e9e as they do not act as Barons, but instead receive a tithe from the Second Estate as a sign of respect.<br /> <br /> Though organised differently to the Lancea et Sanctum in most domains, the First Estate still consider themselves to be part of the Church of Longinus, and indeed claim a continuous tradition from the earliest days of the movement in Rome. They believe it is their God-given duty to weed out the unfaithful and torment the innocent to allow them to prove that their devotion to the Lord is strong. Indeed la clerg\u00e9 are so convinced of this that they have embraced the ideal on a larger scale than is usual, influencing mortal policy and activity directly in order to spread misery and torment thousands. According to local legend (and the First Estate refuse to confirm or deny such rumours) the cycle of plague that wracked the city and kept the population in check, but caused horrors untold was created by la clerg\u00e9 themselves. Supposedly they had a kindred with unique powers to spread filth and infection, and it was only when they realised they could create greater suffering by letting the population grow that they stopped utilising his services.<br /> <br /> The Testament of Longinus remains the foundational work of the devoted kindred in Paris, but it is joined by the so called <i>Letters of Germain</i> which are seen as nearly as important and circulated amongst the dead widely. These letters, supposedly penned by the first Bishop of Paris in the 5<sup>th</sup> century were half of a long and extremely involved discussion between the Bishop and his counterpart in Rome who was at the time the supposed leader of the church, and who had personally overseen the fall of the Camarilla. Though many kindred have looked for them, none of the replies have ever been found, but the philosophy and theology put forwards in what does remain has been very formative. It is an emotive and spiritual appeal to the duty of those who follow the Temple and Spear to cleave to traditional above all things, as to preserve the holy truth as revealed through struggle is to understand the work of God. Many members of the covenant in the city have taken these words to mean that they should push themselves even when it would be more prudent to conserve strength, as by doing so they reveal their true faith as they reveal the faith in others through torment.<br /> <br /> The other theological oddity revealed in the letters has caused some consternation in member of the Lancea et Sanctum in nearby cities as it relies upon a metaphysical concept that is not universally accepted. Specifically, members of la clerg\u00e9 (or at least those of a traditional persuasion) believe that the torment of mortals is not only useful in that it weeds out the unfaithful, but also because it reduces the amount of time that they must stay in purgatory. By working off their sins through punishment in this world they avoid such lengthy punishment in purgatory in the next, and thus can ascend to heaven more quickly and without shedding so much weight of sin. This has even formalised amongst some kindred into a belief that the kindred on earth are in fact the direct agents of God who act as God\u2019s representatives do in purgatory, and that their torment is not simply holy, but actually divine. Members of Les Puret\u00e9 find this stance to be unfathomably offensive, and it is one of the major sources of division between them and their more traditional associates.<br /> <br /> The First Estate is led by \u00c9v\u00eaque (Bishop) Chastain who is the formal advisor of the First Estate to Le Requin. The position of Bishop is one steeped in history in Paris as they are second only to Le Requin in power. According to tradition, should Le Requin be unable to serve for a short period (but not release his praxis) such as when visiting another city, or if engaged in other matters, it is the Bishop who rules in his stead. As such Chastain has a variable amount of power: at times he is little more than a glorified vizier, but at others he is literally a tyrant whose word is law.<br /> <br /> Despite this closeness the relationship between the First Estate and the office of Le Requin, the relationship between Chastain and Henri Rome has soured over the last few decades and the power structure of the city is not nearly as unified as it was under his predecessor. The major reason for this division is a differing attitude to final death and to diablerie. Chastain takes the traditional sanctified stance favouring torpor as a punishment and rejecting final death as it prevents kindred from doing God\u2019s work by ending their eternal suffering, whilst Le Requin is much more liberal with death sentences, imposing them on any law breaker he sees as a threat to his rule. In addition, Rome has on occasion ruled that a criminal who is treasonous in an extraordinary way be subject to amaranth (usually carried out by himself or his sire Amaury Rome), and the diablerist be exempt from all repercussion or criminality for their act. The last kindred to be executed in this manner was Christophe Lapointe, an action which caused considerable division between Le Requin and \u00c9v\u00eaque Chastain.<br /> <br /> <i>Members of the First Estate may learn Theban Sorcery with the storyteller\u2019s approval, though doing so can attract the ire of the Bishop. They are also considered members of the Lancea et Sanctum for the purposes of qualifying for merits, etc.</i> <b>Sects</b><br /><br /> The old guard rule almost supreme in Paris, and as such the members who identify as <b>Catholique</b> dominate the estate. Though many were indeed Roman Catholics in life, the term does not refer to the Church of Holy Rome (which is, of course, superseded by the Temple and Spear in death) but instead uses the word to indicate a continuity of faith and practice, drawing on the rituals and observances of the earliest days of their order. They indeed trace their lineage to Rome, claiming to have established themselves in the city during its time as Lutetia, and their sheer influence makes it believable. All of the high clergy in the city consider themselves catholique, and it is not unpopular amongst the low either.<br /> <br /> Some do throw off the shackles of history, though, and declare themselves part of <b>La R\u00e9forme</b>. These individuals still value the teachings and traditions of their faith, but wish to break away from the constraints of the Estates seeing them as an imposition by the secular world on God and his work. The handful of kindred who identify with this faction would seem to be excellent allies of the burgeoning Radical movement but in truth they want to sweep away all secular governance regardless of its type and replace it with an overt theocracy. Despite this they sometimes pander to the underground movement and pamphleteers in order to gain advantage in the city.<br /> <br /> The last, and smallest, of the sects found amongst le clerg\u00e9 are <b>Les Puret\u00e9</b> who reject the Catholique stance of honouring the religious past completely, and instead advocate adopting a new more puritanical approach to their religious duties. They either were embraced from families which followed mortal protestant faith, or decided to go against the grain after death, finding the pomp and circumstance of the mainstream distasteful. Though few in number Les Puret\u00e9 continue to survive due to their influence beyond the boundaries of the city, and in particular have a strong relationship with like-minded individuals in Britain and Germany. This means they can sometimes accomplish things that others cannot, and so their positions remain secure for now. Despite this, the idea of any of the high clergy being openly puritanical is laughable.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/33-nifara","name":"Nifara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar33_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"38"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-01T16:34:03-08:00","id":424645,"title":"","content":"I have a 1890s crossover setting underway in the same city, though focusing more on the aftermath of the Commune. I'd like to point out that the accusations against Marie Antoinette involved the bloodletting of children abducted from the Parisian streets to keep her beautiful (blood baths, like Bathory). This rumor would turn up numerous times in the 18th century, starting I believe during the rule of the Regent a generation earlier.<br /> <br /> Also, Ventrue is the French &quot;ventru&quot; or belly, but in 18th century slang it means the private parts, particularly of woman. The &quot;ventru&quot; of Paris is said to be Les Halles, the great grocery of central Paris.<br /> <br /> You should also play up the Palais Royal - really the center of much of 18th century Paris' underworld and pre-Revolutionary entertainment, along with the Precincts of the Temple, which were extraterritorial (they belonged to the Order of Malta and were outside the jurisdiction of the police, based at the Chatelet).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1966-jeffjerwin","name":"Jeffjerwin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1966_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"196"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-10T20:59:37-08:00","id":1194132,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jeffjerwin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/408983-fanmade-dark-era-libert\u00e9-\u00e9galit\u00e9-vitae?p=424645#post424645\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Also, Ventrue is the French &quot;ventru&quot; or belly, but in 18th century slang it means the private parts, particularly of woman. The &quot;ventru&quot; of Paris is said to be Les Halles, the great grocery of central Paris.</div> </div> </div> </div>Les Halles were called &quot;le ventre de Paris&quot;, not ventru (see Emile Zola). For definitions of Ventrue, see <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/248439-ventrue-weakness?p=1194131#post1194131\">http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/m...31#post1194131</a> .<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23992-tazmaniacs","name":"Tazmaniacs","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"46"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-09T21:59:39-07:00","id":1205094,"title":"","content":"I come late to the party but very cool, thanks for sharing. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/970-gurkhal","name":"Gurkhal","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"863"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-16T07:03:13-07:00","id":1207638,"title":"","content":"Great stuff! Only if Nifara would Edit new lines in sections titles - it would be greatest.<br /> <br /> Also - is not Requiem 2E corebook marks Invictus as First Estate and Lancea Sanctum as Second?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}